The final match of the [[2006 World Cup|2006 FIFA World Cup]], the 18th edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[association football|football]] teams, was played at the [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] in [[Berlin]], Germany, on 9 July 2006, and was contested between [[Italy national football team|Italy]] and [[France national football team|France]]. The event comprised hosts [[Germany national football team|Germany]] and 31 other teams who emerged from [[2006 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], organised by the six [[FIFA confederations]]. The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[2006 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]].

En route to the final, Italy had to deal with the aftermath of the [[Calciopoli]] and public boycotts. Despite this, Italy finished first in [[2006 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]], with two wins and a draw, after which they defeated [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]] in the round of 16, [[Ukraine national football team|Ukraine]] in the quarter-final, and Germany 2–0 in the semi-final. Suffering from underperformance, France finished runner-up of [[2006 FIFA World Cup Group G|Group G]] with one win and two draws, before defeating [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in the round of 16, [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] in the quarter-final, and [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] 1–0 in the semi-final. The final was witnessed by 69,000 spectators in the stadium, with the [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the match being [[Horacio Elizondo]] from Argentina.

Italy won the World Cup after beating France 5–3 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] following a 1–1 draw at the conclusion of [[Extra time (association football)|extra time]]. The match was focused mostly on France's [[Zinedine Zidane]] and Italy's [[Marco Materazzi]]: this was the last match of Zidane's career, they each scored their team's only goal of the game, and they were also involved in an incident in extra time that led to Zidane being sent off for headbutting Materazzi in the chest. The [[2006 FIFA World Cup final#Zidane–Materazzi incident|Zidane–Materazzi incident]] was the subject of much analysis following the match. Italy's [[Andrea Pirlo]] was named the man of the match, and Zidane was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|FIFA World Cup Golden Ball]] as the best player of the tournament.

The final served as a key match in the [[France–Italy football rivalry]], coming after Italy were defeated by France in the [[UEFA Euro 2000 final]]. Italy's victory was their first world title in 24 years, and their fourth overall, putting them one ahead of Germany and only one behind Brazil. For the first time since November 1993, Italy topped the [[FIFA World Rankings]] in February 2007 as a result of the win. With eight [[football players]] from [[Juventus]] in the final, it equalled the record of [[AC Sparta Prague|Sparta Prague]] in [[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]]. Both sides would fail to get past the group stage in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]], becoming the first set of finalists to do so, while Italy became the third World Cup champions to be eliminated from the group phase after Brazil in [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966]] and France in [[2002 FIFA World Cup|2002]]. As of [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026]], this is the last knockout stage match played by Italy in the tournament.

==Background==
===Venue===
[[File:TG ITA-FRA.jpg|thumb|An example of the Adidas +Teamgeist Berlin ball used in the match]]
The [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] in Berlin was used as the venue for the final,<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006">{{cite news|date=9 July 2006|title=L'Italia è Campione del mondo, Francia ko dopo i calci di rigore|url=https://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/finale-mondiale/finale-mondiale.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="De Stefano 2006">{{cite news|last=De Stefano|first=Gaetano|date=9 July 2006|title=Ci incoronano i rigori|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/09/ITALIA-FRANCIA.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> as well as five other matches over the tournament.<ref name="FIFA 2006">{{cite web|date=9 July 2006|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany2006/matches/match/97410064/|title=Italy – France|publisher=FIFA|access-date=9 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412060648/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany2006/matches/match/97410064/|archive-date=12 April 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref> It was also used for three matches at the [[1974 World Cup]].<ref>{{cite web|date=2019|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany1974/groups/|title=1974 FIFA World Cup|publisher=FIFA|access-date=11 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190707004350/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany1974/groups/|archive-date=7 July 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Olympiastadion was built for the [[1936 Summer Olympics]] in the western part of the city.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.worldsoccer.com/features/stadium-guide/stadium-guide-olympiastadion-berlin-362518|title=Stadium Guide: Olympiastadion Berlin|website=WorldSoccer|date=5 June 2015|access-date=9 August 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160827105131/http://www.worldsoccer.com/features/stadium-guide/stadium-guide-olympiastadion-berlin-362518|archive-date=27 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Since 1985, the stadium has hosted the finals of both the [[DFB-Pokal]] and its [[DFB-Pokal (women)|female equivalent]]. The Olympiastadion hosts the [[Internationales Stadionfest]], which was an [[IAAF Golden League]] event from 1998 to 2009. The stadium hosted the [[2009 World Championships in Athletics]].<ref name=Host1>{{cite web|date=4 December 2004|url=http://www.iaaf.org/news/Kind=512/newsId=27957.html|url-status=dead|title=Berlin to host 2009 World Championships|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121022192316/http://www.iaaf.org/news/Kind=512/newsId=27957.html|archive-date=22 October 2012|publisher=[[IAAF]]|access-date=9 August 2016}}</ref> Aside from its use as an Olympic stadium, the Olympiastadion has a strong footballing tradition, having been the home of [[Hertha BSC]] since 1963.<ref>{{cite web|date=2014|url=http://www.herthabsc.de/de/teams/damals-wars-nuernberg/page/4337--45--.html#.V6ovHFQrK00|title=Damals war's: Bundesliga-Auftakt gegen den 'Club'|publisher=Hertha BSC|access-date=9 August 2016|language=de|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160821093238/http://www.herthabsc.de/de/teams/damals-wars-nuernberg/page/4337--45--.html#.V6ovHFQrK00|archive-date=21 August 2016|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Italy–France rivalry===
{{further|France–Italy football rivalry}}
Italy's first official match as a national team was against France on 15 May 1910,<ref>{{cite web|date=2008|url=http://www.magliarossonera.it/190910_album.html|title=Album della stagione|website=Magliarossonera|language=it|access-date=15 June 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081230141227/http://www.magliarossonera.it/190910_album.html|archive-date=30 December 2008|url-status=live}}</ref> and the two countries developed a [[football rivalry]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/luglio/07/Italia_Francia_antiche_ossessioni_rivalita_co_9_060707051.shtml|archive-url=https://archive.today/20150224190737/http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/luglio/07/Italia_Francia_antiche_ossessioni_rivalita_co_9_060707051.shtml|url-status=dead|archive-date=24 February 2015|title=Italia-Francia Le antiche ossessioni le rivalità profonde e più amore che odio|work=Corriere della Sera|date=7 July 2006|language=it}}</ref><ref name="Today.it 2012">{{cite web|url=http://www.today.it/sport/italia-francia-precedenti.html|title=Italia e Francia di nuovo contro dopo 4 anni: storia di una grande rivalità|date=12 November 2012|website=Today.it|language=it|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401152305/http://www.today.it/sport/italia-francia-precedenti.html|archive-date=1 April 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> They have faced each other in several world-stage tournaments, including previous World Cups in [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]] (3–1 Italy),<ref>{{cite web|date=2015|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=429/match=1164/index.html#nosticky|title=Match Report France – Italy|publisher=FIFA|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200103163710/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round%3D429/match%3D1164/index.html#nosticky|archive-date=3 January 2020|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]] (2–1 Italy),<ref>{{cite web|date=2015|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=278/match=2347/index.html|title=Match Report Italy – France|publisher=FIFA|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180626001606/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=278/match=2347/index.html|archive-date=26 June 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]] (2–0 France),<ref>{{cite web|date=2016|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=309/match=568/index.html|title=Match Report Italy – France|publisher=FIFA|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190401164226/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=309/match=568/index.html|archive-date=1 April 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> and [[1998 FIFA World Cup|1998]] (4–3 in penalty shoot-out to France).<ref>{{cite web|date=2015|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=1025/match=8781/index.html|title=Match Report Italy – France|publisher=FIFA|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191215054201/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/matches/round=1025/match=8781/index.html|archive-date=15 December 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> Most recently, they had contested the [[UEFA Euro 2000 final]], which ended in a 2–1 victory for France after a [[golden goal]] in [[Extra time (association football)|extra time]] by [[David Trezeguet]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/euro2000/816194.stm|title=France 2 Italy 1|date=2 July 2000|work=BBC Sport|access-date=19 June 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190726060204/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/euro2000/816194.stm|archive-date=26 July 2019|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/jul/02/euro2000.sport6|title=France 2-1 Italy|date=2 July 2000|work=The Guardian|access-date=28 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304234936/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2000/jul/02/euro2000.sport6|archive-date=4 March 2016|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=2012|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/season=2000/overview/index.html|title=Trezeguet strikes gold for France|website=UEFA.com|access-date=28 February 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151228113608/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuro/season=2000/overview/index.html|archive-date=28 December 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The match was the sixth [[FIFA World Cup final]] for Italy and the second for France.<ref name="FIFA 2007">{{cite web|date=2007|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-301_09a_wc-finals_alltime_8864.pdf|title=FIFA World Cup Finals since 1930|access-date=3 February 2009|publisher=FIFA|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514092024/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mcwc/ip-301_09a_wc-finals_alltime_8864.pdf|archive-date=14 May 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> Italy had won three of their previous finals ([[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]], 1938, [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982]]), losing two ([[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]], [[1994 FIFA World Cup|1994]]), while France had won their only previous final (1998).<ref name="FIFA 2007"/> It was the first final since 1978 in which neither Germany nor Brazil competed and only the second since 1938; it was also the first all-European final since Italy won the World Cup in 1982 and the second final to be decided by a penalty shoot-out (1994 was the first, with Italy losing to Brazil on that occasion).<ref name="FIFA 2007"/>

===Zidane's announced retirement and Serie A scandal===
In April 2006, France's [[Zinedine Zidane]], who at the time played for Spanish league side [[Real Madrid]] and also previously served for the Italian league side Juventus, announced his retirement from football, saying his playing career would end after the World Cup.<ref>{{cite news|last=Baskett|first=Simon|title=Zidane to retire after one last tilt at World Cup success|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/apr/26/newsstory.sport|date=26 April 2006|work=The Guardian|access-date=19 May 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170728123409/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/apr/26/newsstory.sport|archive-date=28 July 2017|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Reuters 2009">{{cite news|date=16 December 2009|title=Pictures of the Decade Part 9|url=https://www.reuters.com/news/picture/pictures-of-the-decade-part-idJPRTXR29D/|agency=Reuters|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref> Considered "one of the greatest footballers ever" and at the time still the "world's most expensive player", his honours list included a [[Ballon d'Or]], three [[FIFA World Player of the Year]] awards, and [[FIFA World Cup]], [[UEFA European Championship]], [[UEFA Champions League]], [[La Liga]], and [[Serie A]] winners' medals.<ref name="Bandini & Stafford 2009">{{cite news|last1=Bandini|first1=Nicky|last2=Stafford|first2=Mikey|date=11 December 2009|title=The Joy of Six: sporting villains of the decade|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2009/dec/11/the-joy-of-six-sporting-villains-decade|work=The Guardian|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref><ref name="ABC News 2009">{{cite news|date=22 December 2009|title=Head-butt rap helps Zidane rest easy|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2009-12-22/head-butt-rap-helps-zidane-rest-easy/1187798|work=ABC News|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref> In May 2006, Italy's Serie A league was hit by ''Calciopoli'',<ref name="Ingram 2021">{{cite web|last=Ingram|first=Sam|date=20 December 2021|url=https://zicoball.com/calciopoli-when-referee-designators-were-the-most-desired-pawns/|url-status=dead|title=Calciopoli Scandal: Referee Designators As Desired Pawns|website=ZicoBall|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221210162402/https://zicoball.com/calciopoli-when-referee-designators-were-the-most-desired-pawns/|archive-date=10 December 2022|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=When the world watched Italy: FIFA World Cup 2006|url=https://www.fifa.com/en/articles/when-the-world-watched-italy-fifa-world-cup-2006|publisher=FIFA|date=15 November 2022|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="Horncastle 2024">{{cite news|last=Horncastle|first=James|date=10 December 2024|title=Juventus, Man City and the far-reaching impact of a scandal that resulted in relegation|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5976120/2024/12/10/juventus-calciopoli-man-city-premier-league-charges/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815213714/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5976120/2024/12/10/juventus-calciopoli-man-city-premier-league-charges/|archive-date=15 August 2025|access-date=25 June 2026|work=The Athletic}}</ref> a controversial scandal mainly surrounding Juventus (judged in rushed proceedings lasting two months and 12 days to make way for the World Cup and the 2006–2007 football season),<ref name="Calabrese 2006">{{cite news|last=Calabrese|first=Giuseppe|date=15 July 2006|url=http://ricerca.repubblica.it/repubblica/archivio/repubblica/2006/07/15/un-incubo-lungo-tre-mesi-per-dimostrare.html|title=Un incubo lungo tre mesi per dimostrare l'innocenza|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200327162703/https://ricerca.repubblica.it/repubblica/archivio/repubblica/2006/07/15/un-incubo-lungo-tre-mesi-per-dimostrare.html|archive-date=27 March 2020|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007">{{cite book|last1=Cambiaghi|first1=Emilio|last2=Dent|first2=Arthur|url=https://download.ju29ro.com/il_processo_illecito.pdf|title=Il processo illecito|publisher=Stampa Indipendente|date=July 2007|edition=1st|language=it|access-date=25 June 2026|via=Ju29ro.com, 13 March 2008}}</ref><ref name="Bucchioni & D'Ascoli 2007">{{cite book|last1=Bucchioni|first1=Enzo|last2=D'Ascoli|first2=Mario|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=vsOoIAAACAAJ|title=Un calcio nel cuore|publisher=TEA|date=October 2007|isbn=978-88-502-1411-2|edition=1st|location=Milan|language=it|access-date=25 June 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref>{{refn|The sports proceedings were controversial,<ref name="Ingram 2021"/><ref name="Horncastle 2024"/> especially in regards to Juventus but also with the ''ad personam'' decision by the then FIGC extrordinary commissioner and former member of the Inter Milan board Guido Rossi and his appointed experts to assign the ''scudetto'' to third-placed Inter Milan.<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Mughini 2008"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> The handling and decisions by sports justice attracted significant criticism, including from the former [[Constitutional Court of Italy]] judge and president Antonio Baldassarre, ''Totonero'' head of the investigation office and former FIGC judge Corrado De Biase, former FIGC judge Giuseppe Benedetto who resigned in protest, journalists like [[Enzo Biagi]] (widely considered a giant of Italian journalism), the future ''[[Linkiesta]]'' editor-in-chief [[Christian Rocca]], and prominent investigative journalist [[Oliviero Beha]] (who had coined the term ''Moggiopoli'' at the outbreak of the scandal and later became a prominent critic of how it was handled),<ref name="Mughini 2008"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref>{{cite news|title=Moggi, De Santis, le intercettazioni: vent'anni dopo, Mirandola riscopre Calciopoli|url=https://www.sulpanaro.net/2026/05/moggi-de-santis-le-intercettazioni-ventanni-dopo-mirandola-riscopre-calciopoli/|work=SulPanaro.net|date=17 May 2026|access-date=28 June 2026|language=it}}</ref> politicians like the former prime minister and president [[Francesco Cossiga]], lawyers like the well known Inter Milan supporter and future Milan mayor [[Giuliano Pisapia]], and former Milan and Italy manager [[Arrigo Sacchi]],<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref>{{cite web|date=2009|title=Commenti autorevoli|url=https://www.giulemanidallajuve.com/newsite/commenti.asp|access-date=25 June 2026|website=Giù le mani dalla Juve|language=it}}</ref> and [[trial by media]] and [[kangaroo court]] accusations, with comparisons to the [[Inquisition]] (especially the [[Spanish Inquisition]]) and [[Examples of totalitarian regimes|totalitarian states]].<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Mughini 2008"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/>|group=nb}} a team to which five of the national squad players belonged,<ref name="Mughini 2008">{{cite book|last=Mughini|first=Giampiero|date=1 February 2008|chapter=II. Il dolente popolo juventino|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2a1BJgAACAAJ|title=Juve. Il sogno che continua|location=Milan|publisher=Mondadori|isbn=978-88-04-57594-8|edition=1st|language=it|pages=39–84|access-date=25 June 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=9 July 2014|title=9 luglio 2006, ItalJuve Mondiale|url=https://www.juventus.com/it/news/articoli/9-luglio-2006-italjuve-mondiale|access-date=25 June 2026|website=Juventus.com|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=8 June 2018|url=http://www.juventus.com/en/news/news/2018/juventus-world-cup-winners.php|title=Juventus World Cup winners|website=Juventus.com|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-date=14 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210714202325/https://www.juventus.com/en/news/articles/juventus-world-cup-winners|url-status=live}}</ref> before in later years it emerged that virtually all Serie A clubs were involved but time-barred,<ref>{{cite web|last=Garganese|first=Carlo|date=17 June 2011|url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/1717/editorial/2011/06/17/2535702/revealed-the-evidence-that-shows-luciano-moggi-is-the-victim|title=Revealed: The Calciopoli evidence that shows Luciano Moggi is the victim of a witch-hunt|website=Goal.com|access-date=25 June 2026|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110618141816/https://www.goal.com/en/news/1717/editorial/2011/06/17/2535702/revealed-the-evidence-that-shows-luciano-moggi-is-the-victim|archive-date=18 June 2011}}</ref><ref name="Prioreschi 2012">{{cite book|last=Prioreschi|first=Maurilio|date=October 2012|chapter=La cronologia di Calciopoli|title=30 sul campo. Tutta l'altra verità di Calciopoli|location=Milan|publisher=Baldini&Castoldi|pages=9–16|isbn=978-88-66-20809-9|edition=1st|language=it}}</ref> causing a controversy between Juventus, [[Inter Milan]], and the [[Italian Football Federation]] (FIGC) that lasted for the next two decades over unequal treatment.<ref name="Zampini 2023">{{cite book|last=Zampini|first=Massimo|chapter=2006. Il calcio rovesciato. Calciopoli in dieci storie, dieci citazioni e un articolo|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=97DlEAAAQBAJ|title=Juventus. Un'ossessione italiana|date=12 December 2023|publisher=Baldini+Castoldi|isbn=979-12-5494-086-0<!-- E-book ed. 979-12-5494-564-3 -->|location=Milan|language=it|edition=1st|pages=101–148|access-date=25 June 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref><ref name="Della Valle 2024">{{cite book|last=Della Valle|first=Fabiana|date=November 2024|chapter=Dalla Champions alla B|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SHcH0QEACAAJ|title=La storia della Juventus. Il racconto bianconero|location=Santarcangelo di Romagna|publisher=DIARKOS|isbn=978-88-3616-389-2|language=it|pages=185–214|access-date=25 June 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref><ref name="Gigantino 2025">{{cite book|last=Gigantino|first=Michele|date=9 November 2025|title=La Signora del calcio italiano. Storia della Juventus|location=Torrazza Piemonte| isbn=979-8273492592|language=it|pages=394–419}}</ref>

In July 2006, [[amnesty]] calls by various individuals, including politicians across the political spectrum in a polarised climate, as had been done in the early 1980s prior to Italy winning its third World Cup,<ref>{{cite news|date=4 July 2006|title=Calciopoli – Berlusconi: altro che Milan, è un attacco politico|url=https://www.lagazzettadelmezzogiorno.it/news/notizie-nascoste/51120/calciopoli-berlusconi-altro-che-milan-e-un-attacco-politico.html|access-date=27 June 2026|work=La Gazzetta del Mezzogiorno|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=11 July 2006|title=Amnistia, la politica si spacca|url=https://www.tgcom24.mediaset.it/sport/articoli/318281/amnistia-la-politica-si-spacca.shtml|access-date=27 June 2026|website=TGcom24|language=it}}</ref> were ultimately rejected in a public and media climate of "[[guilty until proven innocent]]",<ref name="Longhi & Molinari 2023">{{cite journal|last1=Longhi|first1=Lorenzo|last2=Molinari|first2=Alberto|date=December 2023|title=I dieci giorni che sconvolsero il calcio. Il caso calciopoli nella rappresentazione della stampa|url=https://www.bibliomanie.it/?p=10949|journal=Bibliomanie. Letterature, Storiografie, Semiotiche|language=it|volume=56|issue=4|doi=10.48276/issn.2280-8833.10949|issn=2280-8833|access-date=27 June 2026}}</ref> and many were openly cheering against Italy.<ref>{{cite news|last=Hafez|first=Shamoon|date=5 October 2019|title=Calciopoli Scandal That Rocked Italy|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/49910626|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 April 2021|archive-date=2 September 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220902143253/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/49910626|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Caprotti|first=Alberto|date=9 July 2022|title=Quando l'Italia infangata vinceva i Mondiali: 2006, il paradosso della redenzione|url=https://www.avvenire.it/agora/sport/berlino-2006-fango-e-oro-il-paradosso-della-redenzione_109497|work=Avvenire|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Moreover, part of the media and several individuals, including the then minister [[Giovanna Melandri]], had called for Italy [[Manager (association football)|manager]] and Juventus players to be excluded from the World Cup.<ref name="Calabrese 2006"/><ref name="Mughini 2008"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> Lippi and all Juventus players were subsequently cleared of any wrongdoing.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/>{{refn|The [[Calciopoli trials|''Calciopoli'' criminal trials]] in Rome (GEA World) and Naples (club directors) were largely based on [[circumstantial evidence]] and especially the trials in Naples proved controversial,<ref>{{cite journal|last=Notaro|first=Carmelo|date=26 April 2016|title=La vicenda Calciopoli fra incertezze e contraddizioni|journal=La Legislazione Penale|url=https://www.lalegislazionepenale.eu/wp-content/uploads/2016/04/approfondiment_Notaro_2016.pdf|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> and respectively concluded with acquittals and the statute of limitations for all defendants in relations to charges of criminal conspiracy.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> The first-instance trial in Naples had concluded that there had been no [[match fixing]] in favour of Juventus (in line with the sports proceedings) and thus the league standings had not been altered.<ref>{{cite web|last=Beha|first=Oliviero|date=7 February 2012|url=http://notizie.tiscali.it/opinioni/Beha/2535/articoli/Il-caso-Moggi-e-le-colpe-della-stampa-non-fa-inchieste-di-pende-dai-verbali-non-sa-leggere-le-sentenze-e-non-avvicina-fatti-e-notizie-per-approfondire-l-analisi-Vi-basta.html|url-status=dead|title=Il 'caso Moggi' e le colpe della stampa: non fa inchieste, (di)pende dai verbali, non sa leggere le sentenze|website=Tiscali|language=it|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120312224520/http://notizie.tiscali.it/opinioni/Beha/2535/articoli/Il-caso-Moggi-e-le-colpe-della-stampa-non-fa-inchieste-di-pende-dai-verbali-non-sa-leggere-le-sentenze-e-non-avvicina-fatti-e-notizie-per-approfondire-l-analisi-Vi-basta.html|archive-date=12 March 2012|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Rossini|first=Claudio|date=5 March 2014|url=https://it.blastingnews.com/opinioni/2014/03/calciopoli-e-la-verita-di-comodo-0070692.html|title=Calciopoli e la verità di comodo|website=Blasting News|language=it|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Vaciago|first=Guido|date=28 July 2015|url=https://tuttosport.com/news/calcio/serie-a/juventus/calciopoli/2015/07/28-2774593/cassazione_sistema_inquinato_ma_non_spiega_i_misteri_di_calciopoli|title=Cassazione: 'Sistema inquinato'. Ma non spiega i misteri di Calciopoli|work=Tuttosport|language=it|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref> This latter point contradicted the decision of sports justice, leading to calls for a revision of the decision by sports justice.<ref name="Vaciago 2015">{{cite news|last=Vaciago|first=Guido|date=22 January 2015|title=Calciopoli, oggi Cassazione ma il 2006 è già smontato|url=http://www.tuttosport.com/calcio/serie_a/juventus/2015/01/22-318921/Calciopoli,+oggi+Cassazione+ma+il+2006+%C3%A8+gi%C3%A0+smontato|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150124231933/http://www.tuttosport.com/calcio/serie_a/juventus/2015/01/22-318921/Calciopoli,+oggi+Cassazione+ma+il+2006+%C3%A8+gi%C3%A0+smontato|archive-date=24 January 2015|access-date=25 June 2026|work=Tuttosport|language=it|url-status=live}}</ref> According to the sports proceedings, that Juventus had altered the league standings, even without fixing a single match (a logic strongly criticised),<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> with no direct Article 6 violation warranting relegation,<ref name="Ingram 2021"/> significantly aggravated the club's position,<!-- Calciopoli experts --><ref name="Arpino 2018">{{cite web|last=Arpino|first=Felix|date=29 May 2018|title=Calciopoli: l'illecito sportivo della Juventus|url=https://ilcalcioeugualepertutti.wordpress.com/2018/05/29/calciopoli-lillecito-sportivo-della-juventus/|access-date=27 June 2026|website=Il calcio è uguale per tutti|language=it}}</ref> and thus it caused the controversial relegation of Juventus to [[Serie B]] for the first time in its history and the revocation of the two ''[[scudetti]]'' won by the club on the pitch,<ref name="Ingram 2021"/><ref name="Horncastle 2024"/> including one that was never an object of investigation and that was assigned to third-placed Inter Milan, which was subsequently heavily implicated in the scandal.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> This and other key arguments that were instrumental in the 2006 decision were ultimately rebuked by the criminal trials.<ref name="Prioreschi 2012"/><ref name="Vaciago 2015"/> This specific part of the reasonings (no match fixing and league standings not altered) was definitely confirmed by Italy's [[Supreme Court of Cassation (Italy)|Supreme Court of Cassation]] in 2015 and 2025.<ref>{{cite web|last=Santoro|first=Luca|date=29 March 2025|title=Calciopoli, la Cassazione dà ragione alla Juventus: niente risarcimento al Bologna. Sui social nuovi attacchi all'Inter|url=https://sport.virgilio.it/calciopoli-la-cassazione-da-ragione-alla-juventus-niente-risarcimento-al-bologna-sui-social-nuovi-attacchi-all-inter-903200|access-date=25 June 2026|website=Virgilio Sport|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=29 March 2025|title=La Cassazione mette fine a Calciopoli: 'Nessun risultato alterato', ma il calcio italiano riflette ancora|url=https://www.ciociariaoggi.it/news/sport-nazionale/307022/la-cassazione-mette-fine-a-calciopoli-nessun-risultato-alterato-ma-il-calcio-italiano-riflette-ancora.html|access-date=25 June 2026|work=Ciociara Oggi|language=it}}</ref>|group=nb}} That so many players in the final were from Juventus was cited by club supporters and commentators, including some critics of the club and other figures, such as the then FIGC president [[Franco Carraro]],<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Capuano|first=Giovanni|date=17 March 2015|url=https://www.panorama.it/sport/carraro-campionato-1998-falsato-juventus-iuliano-ronaldo-rigore-moggi|url-status=live|title=La memoria ritardata di Carraro: 'Campionato '98 falsato'. Quando era nº1 di Lega...|magazine=Panorama|language=it|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208235604/https://www.panorama.it/sport/carraro-campionato-1998-falsato-juventus-iuliano-ronaldo-rigore-moggi|archive-date=8 December 2022|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=12 December 2020|url=https://www.sportevai.it/calcio/carraro-calciopoli-colpa-mia-ma-juve-avrebbe-vinto-uguale.html|url-status=live|title=Carraro: Calciopoli colpa mia ma Juve avrebbe vinto uguale|website=Sport e Vai|language=it|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221208235613/https://www.sportevai.it/calcio/carraro-calciopoli-colpa-mia-ma-juve-avrebbe-vinto-uguale.html|archive-date=8 December 2022|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://sport.virgilio.it/moggi-torna-su-calciopoli-e-carraro-forse-si-e-redento-654133|title=Moggi torna su Calciopoli e Carraro: Forse si è redento|website=Virgilio Sport|language=it|date=17 September 2020|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref> the then Juventus manager [[Fabio Capello]], and the then Juventus player [[Zlatan Ibrahimović]],<ref name="Horncastle 2024"/> to argue that Juventus was the strongest team and had legitimately won on the pitch back-to-back the [[2004–2005 Serie A]] and the [[2005–2006 Serie A]] revoked league titles.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/>{{refn|Marcello Lippi had been a successful manager at Juventus from 1994 to 1999 and from 2001 to 2004,<ref>{{cite news|last=Ranieri|first=Claudio|date=9 July 2006|title=Lippi makes right calls and wins respect|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/09/worldcup2006.sport|work=The Guardian|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> when he took over the role as manager of Italy.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> Among Juventus players in the final were Gianluigi Buffon, Gianluca Zambrotta, Fabio Cannavaro, and Mauro Camoranesi as starters for Italy, with club captain and legend Alessandro Del Piero as substitute and starter Simone Perrotta as a former player, and Lilian Thuram and Patrick Viera as starters for France, with David Trezequet as substitute.<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> Moreover, France starters Zinedine Zidane and Thierry Henry had both been at Juventus in the late 1990s and Italy players, such as Fabio Grosso, Andrea Pirlo, Luca Toni (starters), and Vincenzo Iaquinta (substitute) would all move to Juventus in subsequent years, while other key Juventus players in the 2006 World Cup were the 2003 Ballon d'Or winner [[Pavel Nedvěd]] for Czech Republic and Zlatan Ibrahimović for [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]].<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> Among Italy technical staff, an often cited figure from Juventus was the masseur Aldo Esposito, further underling the contributions of Juventus, which as of the [[2022 World Cup]] was the club with the largest number of players to have won the FIFA World Club,<ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Della Valle 2024"/><ref name="Gigantino 2025"/> extending its lead.<ref>{{cite web|date=18 December 2022|title=Le squadre con più campioni del mondo: Juventus sempre più prima!|url=https://www.juventus.com/it/news/articoli/le-squadre-con-piu-campioni-del-mondo-juventus|access-date=25 June 2026|website=Juventus.com|language=it}}</ref> When including the manager, the technical staff, and all the players acquired by the then Juventus management ([[Luciano Moggi]] as general manager, Antonio Giraudo as CEO, and former Juventus captain and club legend [[Roberto Bettega]] as vice-chairman), which brought the club to success and dominance since 1994 and reached both its apotheosis and its fall in 2006, the final had at least 17 individuals linked to Juventus.<ref>{{cite web|date=19 September 2025|title=Calciopoli o Farsopoli? Una ricostruzione|url=https://home.ju29ro.com/articoli/farsopoli/336-calciopoli-o-farsopoli-una-ricostruzione|access-date=25 June 2026|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it}}</ref>|group=nb}} While some individuals like the then FIGC extraordinary commissioner [[Guido Rossi]] publicly supported Lippi, many noted the hypocrisy of those who celebrated the win of Italy when they had cheered against the national squad or had called for Juventus figures to be excluded (including Lippi, who ultimately did not renew his managerial contract as a result of this), explicitily citing the case of Melandri.<ref name="Mughini 2008"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/>

