===Squads analysis===
[[File:2006 FIFA World Cup - Italy - Buffon, Materazzi and Perrotta (edited).jpg|thumb|left|Italian players [[Gianluigi Buffon]], [[Marco Materazzi]], and [[Simone Perrotta]] celebrate Italy's qualification for the round of 16 at the end of the group stage match against the [[Czech Republic national football team|Czech Republic]].]]
Italy and France met again six years after the Euro 2000 final and eight years after their last meeting in a World Cup tournament, the quarter-finals of the 1998 World Cup, both of which had been won by France. Once the final tournament began, Italy managed to isolate themselves from the aforementioned scandal and reached the knockout stage without major difficulties, following a group stage campaign in Group E that saw them secure two wins and a draw, respectively against Ghana (2–0), the United States (1–1), and the Czech Republic (2–0). Nonetheless, these initial matches were not without issues for Lippi's men: [[Daniele De Rossi]] received a four-match ban for elbowing American player [[Brian McBride]],<ref>{{cite news|last=De Stefano|first=Gaetano|date=17 June 2006|title=Italia, pari pieno di rimpianti|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/06_Giugno/17/Italia-Usa.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> while [[Alessandro Nesta]] ended his World Cup tournament early due to a serious injury sustained during the match against the Czechs.<ref>{{cite news|last=Apicella|first=Nicola|date=22 June 2006|title=Rep. Ceca-Italia 0-2 (Conclusa)|url=http://www.repubblica.it/speciale/2006/mondiali/dati/dirette/1596550.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

In the round of 16, the ''Azzurri'' struggled more than expected to defeat Australia (1–0), prevailing only thanks to a penalty converted by [[Francesco Totti]] in stoppage time,<ref>{{cite news|last=De Stefano|first=Gaetano|date=26 June 2006|title=Azzurri da batticuore: 1-0 al 95'|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/06_Giugno/26/ita-aus.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> while the comfortable quarter-final victory over [[Andriy Shevchenko]]'s Ukraine (3–0) went according to predictions.<ref>{{cite news|last=De Stefano|first=Gaetano|date=30 June 2006|title=E adesso la Germania|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/06_Giugno/30/italiaucraina.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> In the semi-final, the hosts and three-time world champions Germany succumbed to the ''Azzurri'' (0–2) following a late one-two punch by [[Fabio Grosso]] and [[Alessandro Del Piero]] at the very end of extra time.<ref>{{cite news|last=De Stefano|first=Gaetano|date=4 July 2006|title=Italia gran finale|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/04/GERMANIA-ITALIA.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

A defining characteristic of the Italian squad was the continuous rotation of starting players. With the exception of the two backup goalkeepers, [[Angelo Peruzzi]] and [[Marco Amelia]], every other selected player was fielded at least once. Throughout the tournament, the attacking line-up alternated between Del Piero, [[Alberto Gilardino]], [[Vincenzo Iaquinta]], [[Filippo Inzaghi]], [[Luca Toni]], and Totti, supported by a solid midfield and above all a nearly impenetrable defence (conceding only one goal, which was an [[own goal]]), with goalkeeper [[Gianluigi Buffon]] remaining unbeaten for 453 minutes.<ref name="Gualerzi 2006"/> After the semi-final with Germany, [[Gianni Mura]], one of Italy's leading sports journalists, celebrated the ''Azzurri'' by associating each player with a colour and a wine. He compared Materazzi to [[Nero d'Avola]] with these words: "Here too, the assonance has played a part. It is a wine of resolute character that has boosted the reputation of bottled Sicilian wine. If you do not watch your step, it will floor you. Just like Materazzi, precisely."<ref>{{cite web|last=Zavagli|first=Massimo|date=22 March 2020|title=Calcio e vino, gli amori di Gianni Mura|url=https://www.oscarwine.it/calcio-e-vino-gli-amori-di-gianni-mura/|website=Oscarwine|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

[[File:WM06 Portugal-France Penalty.jpg|thumb|[[Zinedine Zidane]]'s penalty against [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]], which sealed France's qualification for the final in Berlin.]]
The ''Bleus'' secured second place in Group G with some difficulty, recording two draws and a victory against Switzerland (0–0), South Korea (1–1), and Togo (2–0). Even during these first three matches, France displayed physical and technical limitations, and Domenech was not immune to criticism: among other things, the manager made no secret of aligning the team partly based on astrological signs,<ref>{{cite news|last=Perrone|first=Roberto|date=18 June 2006|title=Domenech Oroscopo e relazioni pericolose. La Francia condanna il suo c.t.|url=http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/giugno/18/Domenech_Oroscopo_relazioni_pericolose_Francia_co_9_060618148.shtml|work=Corriere della Sera|language=it|page=45|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151101140406/http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/giugno/18/Domenech_Oroscopo_relazioni_pericolose_Francia_co_9_060618148.shtml|archive-date=1 November 2015|url-status=dead}}</ref> and regularly left [[David Trezeguet]]—generally considered one of the finest forwards of his generation—on the bench for both tactical reasons, favouring a system with [[Thierry Henry]] as a lone striker, and specifically for reasons related to astrology.<ref>{{cite news|date=13 June 2006|title=La Francia stecca la prima. 'Bleus' bloccati dalla Svizzera|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/francia-svizzera/francia-svizzera.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

With several players still remaining from the 1998 World Cup victory, the squad was accused of being old and worn out—or "old and toothless", as Spain manager [[Luis Aragonés]] put it.<ref name="La Repubblica, 27 June 2006">{{cite news|date=27 June 2006|title=È il trionfo della 'vecchia' Francia|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/francia-spagna/francia-spagna.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> In the knockout stage, carried by a rejuvenated Zidane (who had been quiet until then), the French overcame Spain (3–1) in the round of 16,<ref name="La Repubblica, 27 June 2006"/> defending champions Brazil (1–0) in the quarter-finals,<ref>{{cite news|date=1 July 2006|title=Zidane è ancora il re. Brasile eliminato|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/brasile-francia/brasile-francia.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> and [[Luís Figo]]'s Portugal in the semi-final (1–0), which featured a young [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] in his first World Cup experience.<ref>{{cite news|date=5 July 2006|title=Rigore Zidane, Francia in finale, sfiderà l'Italia per il Mondiale|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/portogallo-francia/portogallo-francia.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

===Italy===
Italy's campaign in the tournament was accompanied by open pessimism,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://english.people.com.cn/200605/23/eng20060523_267753.html|title=Scandal threatening to bury Italy's Cup dream|website=People's Daily Online|date=23 May 2006|access-date=21 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121019223102/http://english.people.com.cn/200605/23/eng20060523_267753.html|archive-date=19 October 2012|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Brewin & Williamson 2014">{{cite web|last1=Brewin|first=John|last2=Williamson|first2=Martin|date=1 May 2014|title=World Cup History: 2006|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37364051/2006|website=Soccer on ESPN|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> in large part due to the controversy caused by the Serie A scandal.<ref>{{cite news|last=Buckley|first=Kevin|date=21 May 2006|title=Lippi the latest to be sucked into crisis|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/may/21/newsstory.sport2|work=The Guardian|access-date=27 June 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060526072033/http://football.guardian.co.uk/News_Story/0%2C%2C1779716%2C00.html|archive-date=26 May 2006|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Cox|first=Michael|date=8 March 2026|title=How Italy won the 2006 World Cup: Six forwards, Pirlo's control and overcoming Calciopoli|url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7093496/2026/03/08/how-italy-won-the-2006-world-cup/|work=The Athletic|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> Italy arrived at the 2006 World Cup in a decidedly complicated situation.<ref name="Ingram 2021"/><ref name="Horncastle 2024"/> During that year, the world of [[Italian football]] had been heavily shaken by the ''Calciopoli'' scandal, during which a series of relationships between club directors and representatives of the refereeing class had emerged, limited to the 2004–2005 Serie A season.<ref name="Ingram 2021"/><ref name="Horncastle 2024"/> It later emerged that these relations, which were not limited to the Italian league and were formally banned across Europe only in the aftermath of the scandal following the example of Italy,<ref>{{cite web|date=13 March 2008|title=La violazione dell'art. 1 del CGS e la slealtà sportiva. Ante e post calciopoli|url=https://www.ju29ro.com/articoli/farsopoli/189-la-violazione-dellart-1-del-cgs-e-la-slealta-sportiva-ante-e-post-calciopoli|access-date=30 June 2026|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it}}</ref> had been encouraged by the FIGC itself and the then FIGC president Franco Carraro in order to promote good relations among clubs and avoid further controversies, such as complaining in public about referees.<ref name="Bucchioni & D'Ascoli 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> The club most affected by sports justice was Juventus,<ref>{{cite news|title=Sconti: sorridono Juve e Lazio|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Primo_Piano/2006/10_Ottobre/27/arbitratoiniziato.shtml|url-status=dead|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|date=27 October 2006|language=it|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061106211255/http://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Primo_Piano/2006/10_Ottobre/27/arbitratoiniziato.shtml|archive-date=6 November 2006|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> the only club to be relegated to Serie B despite similar violations to the other four Serie A clubs ([[Fiorentina]], [[SS Lazio|Lazio]], [[AC Milan|Milan]], and [[Reggina]]) initially involved in the scandal.<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/>

Perhaps the most famous scandal of [[match-fixing in association football]], such as that the prosecution spoke of many fixed matches and multiple leagues that had been altered through corrupted referees and rigged drawing lots, all of which were widely publicised by the press and taken for granted by the public, the sentences ultimately debunked these allegations, with no [[match fixing]], no involved players (unlike previous and subsequent match-fixing scandals), no corrupted referees, no exchange or trace of money involved, and no rigged drawing lots.<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> Ultimately, ''Calciopoli'' was about alleged violations and early consumation crimes (sporting fraud) that instead revolved around the referee grids, which were discussed between the referee designators and most clubs (which was allowed by the FIGC or at least it was not explicitily against the rules at the time), did not affect the game as they were significantly preliminary (as also found by the Public Prosecutor's Office of Turin in 2004) and there would still be a drawing lot procedure (found to be regular), and were easily predictable due to the preclusion rules.<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Bucchioni & D'Ascoli 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/> As often happened in the history of the ''Azzurri'', also in this case the ''Bianconeri'' represented the backbone of the Italian national team, managed on this occasion by Marcello Lippi, a historic ex-Juventus manager; Lippi himself, with a son (Davide Lippi) who was an agent linked to the [[GEA World]], a [[sports agent]] company that was implicated in the scandal but whose members were ultimately acquitted from [[criminal conspiracy]] charges in the criminal trials in Rome,<ref>{{cite web|date=8 January 2009|title=Il misero crollo della prima cupola|url=http://www.ju29ro.com/farsopoli/1-farsopoli/789-il-misero-crollo-della-prima-cupola.html|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170206140053/http://www.ju29ro.com/farsopoli/1-farsopoli/789-il-misero-crollo-della-prima-cupola.html|archive-date=6 February 2017|access-date=30 June 2026|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=15 January 2014|title=Lo sbriciolamento della prima cupola|url=https://www.ju29ro.com/5442-lo-sbriciolamento-della-prima-cupola|access-date=30 June 2026|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it}}</ref> was the target of a media campaign aimed at pushing him to resign for reasons of expediency,<ref>{{cite news|last=Sorrentino|first=Andrea|date=12 May 2006|title=Lippi, il partito delle dimissioni: 'Il ct faccia un passo indietro'|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/05/sezioni/sport/calcio/lippi-partito-dimissioni/lippi-partito-dimissioni/lippi-partito-dimissioni.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> with many even calling for Italy's exclusion from the World Cup.<ref>{{cite web|last=Panella|first=Luigi|date=10 November 2022|title=Mondiali di calcio 2006: Italia campione del mondo. Il rigore di Grosso e Cannavaro con la coppa al cielo|url=https://www.repubblica.it/sport/calcio/esteri/2022/11/10/news/mondiali_calcio_2006_italia_campione_lippi_grosso_cannavaro-373918969/|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

Italy were drawn in [[2006 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]] alongside [[Ghana national football team|Ghana]], the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]], and [[Czech Republic national football team|Czech Republic]], opening against Ghana on 12 June 2006. Italy took the lead through midfielder [[Andrea Pirlo]] in the 40th minute, eventually winning the match by a score of 2–0.<ref>{{cite news|last=Dampf|first=Andrew|date=12 June 2006|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/06/12/AR2006061201171.html|work=The Washington Post|access-date=5 May 2010|title=Pirlo Leads Italy Past Ghana at World Cup|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110513231811/http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/06/12/AR2006061201171.html|archive-date=13 May 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> In their next match against the United States on 17 June, Italy took the lead in the 22nd minute with an [[Alberto Gilardino]] goal, but five minutes later [[Cristian Zaccardo]] scored an [[own goal]] off an attempted clearance following a free-kick, and the match eventually remained a 1–1 draw; this was one of only two goals Italy conceded throughout the tournament.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/italia-stati-uniti/italia-stati-uniti.html|title=Italia-Usa: la guerra che non-si voleva Pari con 3 espulsi. Qualificazione rinviata|work=La Repubblica|language=it|date=17 June 2006|access-date=16 March 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150403015335/http://www.repubblica.it/2006/06/speciale/altri/2006mondialipartite/italia-stati-uniti/italia-stati-uniti.html|archive-date=3 April 2015|url-status=live}}</ref> In that match, [[Daniele De Rossi]] received a straight [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)|red card]] after he elbowed [[Brian McBride]] in the face; he left the pitch bloodied, but returned after treatment, later receiving three stitches.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853182.stm|title=Italy 1–1 USA|work=BBC Sport|date=17 June 2006|access-date=10 February 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120209222510/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853182.stm|archive-date=9 February 2012|url-status=live}}</ref> De Rossi later apologised to McBride, who subsequently praised him as "classy" for approaching him after the match.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://soccer.sportsnet.tsn.ca/news_story/?id=246|title=De Rossi apologizes to McBride for elbow|publisher=TSN|access-date=25 July 2016|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929174054/http://soccer.sportsnet.tsn.ca/news_story/?id=246|archive-date=29 September 2007}}</ref> Because of the incident, De Rossi was banned for four matches and was fined [[Swiss franc|CHF]] 10,000.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=372274&cc=5901|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090211074318/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=372274&cc=5901|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 February 2009|title=Italy's De Rossi gets four-match ban from FIFA|date=23 June 2006|access-date=10 February 2012|website=ESPN SoccerNet}}</ref> Their third and final group stage match was against Czech Republic on 22 June. [[Marco Materazzi]], who had begun the tournament as a reserve player, came on as a replacement for [[Alessandro Nesta]] who suffered an injury in the match.<ref>{{cite news|last=Thomas|first=Russell|date=26 June 2006|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jun/26/worldcup2006.sport9|title=Business as usual for Italy despite concern over Nesta|work=The Guardian|access-date=20 October 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181020094956/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jun/26/worldcup2006.sport9|archive-date=20 October 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Materazzi went on to score a goal in the match, and was named Man of the Match of an eventual 2–0 win, finishing top of the group with seven points.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853358.stm|title=Czech Republic 0–2 Italy|work=BBC Sport|date=22 June 2006|access-date=14 December 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151222162421/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853358.stm|archive-date=22 December 2015|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the round of 16 on 26 June, Italy took on [[Australia men's national soccer team|Australia]] in a match in which Materazzi was controversially sent off in the 53rd minute after an attempted two-footed tackle on Australian midfielder [[Mark Bresciano]]. In stoppage time, a controversial penalty kick was awarded to Italy when referee [[Luis Medina Cantalejo]] ruled that [[Lucas Neill]] fouled [[Fabio Grosso]]. [[Francesco Totti]] converted the kick into the upper corner of the goal past [[Mark Schwarzer]] for a 1–0 win.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991534.stm|title=Italy 1–0 Australia|date=26 June 2006|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160105221857/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991534.stm|archive-date=5 January 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> FIFA president [[Sepp Blatter]] later conceded that the penalty decision was a refereeing mistake and apologised to Australian fans, saying that he believed there was "too much cheating on the players' side" and that Australia should have reached the quarter-finals instead of Italy.<ref>{{cite news|date=29 October 2006|title=Blatter apologises for Grosso penalty|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2006/10/29/blatter-apologises-for-grosso-penalty|access-date=26 September 2024|agency=Al Jazeera}}</ref> In the quarter-final on 30 June, Italy took on [[Ukraine national football team|Ukraine]], and [[Gianluca Zambrotta]] opened the scoring early in the 6th minute with a left-footed shot from outside the penalty area after a quick exchange with Totti created enough space. [[Luca Toni]] added two more goals for Italy in the second half, but as Ukraine pressed forward, they were unable to score. Ukraine had hit the crossbar, had several shots saved by Italy goalkeeper [[Gianluigi Buffon]], and were denied a goal from a goal-line clearance from Zambrotta, ultimately ending in a 3–0 win for Italy.<ref>{{cite news|title=Lippi dedicates win to Pessotto|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/italy/5123318.stm|work=BBC Sport|date=30 June 2006|access-date=25 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071227050828/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/italy/5123318.stm|archive-date=27 December 2007|url-status=live}}</ref> In the semi-final on 4 July, Italy beat hosts Germany 2–0 with the two goals coming in the last two minutes of extra time. After a back-and-forth half-hour of extra time, during which Gilardino and Zambrotta struck the post and the crossbar respectively, Grosso scored in the 119th minute after a disguised Pirlo pass found him open in the penalty area for a bending left-footed shot into the far corner past German goalkeeper [[Jens Lehmann]]'s dive. Substitute striker [[Alessandro Del Piero]] then sealed the victory by scoring with a right-footed shot, avenging his mistake in the 2000 Euro final and a period of underperformance with the national squad,<ref name="Brewin & Williamson 2014"/><ref>{{cite news|last=Guerriero-Davies|first=Tom|date=27 June 2016|title=How misses, a last-minute equaliser and a golden goal broke Italy at Euro 2000|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/the-gentleman-ultra/2016/jun/27/italy-france-euro-2000-final-heartbreak|work=The Guardian|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Romano|first=Andrea|date=11 July 2021|title=L'urlo di Tardelli, l'esultanza di Pertini, il rigore di Grosso, lo sguardo fisso di Baggio: lo sbloccaricordi, ovvero le finali dell'Italia in una foto|url=https://www.ilfattoquotidiano.it/2021/07/11/lurlo-di-tardelli-lesultanza-di-pertini-il-rigore-di-grosso-lo-sguardo-fisso-di-baggio-lo-sbloccaricordi-ovvero-le-finali-dellitalia-in-una-foto/6256391/8/|work=Il Fatto Quotidiano|access-date=28 June 2026|language=it}}</ref> in what was the last kick of the game at the end of a swift counterattack by [[Fabio Cannavaro]], Totti, and Gilardino.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991640.stm|title=Germany 0–2 Italy (aet)|date=4 July 2006|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090419001247/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991640.stm|archive-date=19 April 2009|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Juventus profile {{!}} FIFA Club World Cup 2025|url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/club-world-cup/usa-2025/articles/juventus-club-profile-history-players-qualified|publisher=FIFA|date=30 November 2024|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref>

===France===
France was going through a difficult time on the sporting side. After being eliminated from the [[UEFA Euro 2004]] by [[Otto Rehhagel]]'s outsider [[Greece national football team|Greece]], the eventual winners of the tournament, manager [[Jacques Santini]] had been removed from the ''Bleus'' bench and replaced by Raymond Domenech, who was charged with carrying out a generational change that could no longer be postponed; however, the first qualifying matches for the 2006 World Cup had given inconsistent results for France, who managed to obtain the qualification only thanks to the subsequent return to the team of veterans [[Claude Makélélé]], [[Lilian Thuram]], and [[Zinédine Zidane]], who had previously announced their retirement from the national team on several occasions.<ref>{{cite news|last=Crosetti|first=Maurizio|title=Voilà Domenech, il più bistrattato dei ct|url=http://ricerca.repubblica.it/repubblica/archivio/repubblica/2006/07/03/voila-domenech-il-piu-bistrattato-dei-ct.html|work=La Repubblica|date=3 July 2006|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> On the eve of the tournament, rumours also circulated of a divided dressing room, as well as of a bad relationship between Domenech and some players.<ref>{{cite news|last=Gualerzi|first=Valerio|date=3 July 2006|title=Domenech, l'astrologo di Francia tra i rudi della pampa e l'oroscopo|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/speciale/mondiali/servizi/domenech-francia/domenech-francia/domenech-francia.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

France were drawn in [[2006 FIFA World Cup Group G|Group G]] alongside [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]], [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]], and [[Togo national football team|Togo]], opening against Switzerland on 13 June 2006. The match ended in a scoreless draw.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4852856.stm|title=France 0–0 Switzerland|work=BBC Sport|date=13 June 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080516045509/http://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4852856.stm|archive-date=16 May 2008|url-status=live}}</ref> In their next match against South Korea on 18 June, France took the lead in the 9th minute after [[Thierry Henry]] picked up [[Sylvain Wiltord]]'s deflected shot. Later, a header by [[Patrick Vieira]] crossed the goal-line after being blocked by Korean goalkeeper [[Lee Woon-Jae]], but referee [[Benito Archundia]] did not give the goal as he had deemed Vieira to have committed a foul. With about 10 minutes left, [[Park Ji-sung]] scored for Korea to claim an eventual 1–1 draw.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853220.stm|title=France 1–1 South Korea|work=BBC Sport|date=18 June 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110913084041/http://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853220.stm|archive-date=13 September 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> Their third and final group stage match was against Togo on 23 June, and France needed a victory to progress from the group stage. After a goalless first half, Vieira and Henry scored two second half goals within six minutes of each other to win 2–0, and finish second in the group with five points.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853462.stm|title=Togo 0–2 France|work=BBC Sport|date=23 June 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110913080713/http://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4853462.stm|archive-date=13 September 2011|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the round of 16 on 27 June, France took on [[Spain national soccer team|Spain]] in a match in which Spain took the lead in the first half with a penalty kick converted by [[David Villa]] after Thuram fouled [[Pablo Ibáñez|Pablo]]. Four minutes before half time, [[Franck Ribéry]] equalised the score, and with seven minutes before the end of regulation time, Vieira scored from a header for France to take the lead. As Spain pushed forward to find an equaliser, Zidane scored in a solo effort in stoppage time, for a final score of 3–1 for France.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991524.stm|title=Spain 1–3 France|work=BBC Sport|date=27 June 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110913080507/http://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991524.stm|archive-date=13 September 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> In the quarter-final on 1 July, France took on [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]]; France won with a lone goal in the 57th minute by Henry after he volleyed a Zidane free kick to end Brazil's reign as world champions.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991616.stm|title=Brazil 0–1 France|work=BBC Sport|date=1 July 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110913080523/http://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991616.stm|archive-date=13 September 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> In the semi-final against [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] on 5 July, France won again with a lone goal, this time from a first half Zidane penalty kick after Henry was tripped inside the box by [[Ricardo Carvalho]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://newsimg.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991632.stm|title=Portugal 0–1 France|work=BBC Sport|date=5 July 2006}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
The official match ball for the final was the + Teamgeist Berlin, a gold-coloured variation of the [[Adidas Teamgeist]], which was unveiled on 18 April 2006.<ref name="FIFA, 18 April 2006">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/golden-teamgeist-berlin-ball-for-the-fifa-world-cup-final-19615|title=Golden 'Teamgeist Berlin' ball for the FIFA World Cup Final|publisher=FIFA|date=18 April 2006|access-date=12 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190517103240/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/golden-teamgeist-berlin-ball-for-the-fifa-world-cup-final-19615|archive-date=17 May 2019|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Adidas Teamgeist was used as the official match ball throughout the tournament, provided by German sports equipment company [[Adidas]].<ref name="FIFA, 18 April 2006"/> On 6 July 2006, Argentine [[Horacio Elizondo]] was chosen as referee for the final, ahead of German [[Markus Merk]] and Slovak [[Ľuboš Micheľ]].<ref name="BBC Sport, 6 July 2006">{{cite news|title=Elizondo chosen as Cup final ref|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/5152952.stm|work=BBC Sport|date=6 July 2006|access-date=6 July 2006|archive-date=14 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210714202300/https://secure-uk.imrworldwide.com/v51.js|url-status=live}}</ref> Elizondo became a referee in 1994 and refereed his first international match in 1996.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.fifa.com/en/development/refereeing/index/0,1247,72546,00.html?articleid=72546|url-status=dead|title=Horacio Elizondo: 'Referees need to be clinical'|date=4 November 2003|publisher=FIFA|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060719062459/http://www.fifa.com/en/development/refereeing/index/0,1247,72546,00.html?articleid=72546|archive-date=19 July 2006|access-date=1 June 2006}}</ref> His compatriots, Darío García and Rodolfo Otero, were chosen as assistant referees,<ref name="BBC Sport, 6 July 2006"/> and the Spanish Luis Medina Cantalejo as [[fourth official]].<ref name="ESPN SoccerNet 2006">{{cite web|title=Fourth Official: I saw Zidane's Headbutt|website=ESPN SoccerNet|date=11 July 2006|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373704&cc=5901|access-date=11 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060718093708/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373704&cc=5901|archive-date=18 July 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref> Elizondo had given England striker [[Wayne Rooney]] a red card against Portugal previously in the tournament.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/england/5130880.stm|title=Rooney's dismissal stuns England|work=BBC Sport|date=1 July 2006|access-date=1 July 2006}}</ref> Given the presence of four European semi-finalists, the FIFA refereeing committee deemed that refereeing crews for the final four matches should not consist of officials from that continent. For Elizondo, it was his fifth match of the 2006 World Cup and his first officiating either of the two finalists.<ref>{{cite news|last=Monti|first=Fabio|url=http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/luglio/07/finale_Elizondo_arbitro_poeta_co_9_060707050.shtml|title=La finale a Elizondo, l'arbitro-poeta|work=Corriere della Sera|date=7 July 2006|page=57|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151117032412/http://archiviostorico.corriere.it/2006/luglio/07/finale_Elizondo_arbitro_poeta_co_9_060707050.shtml|archive-date=17 November 2015|url-status=dead|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

Italy's team doctor Enrico Castellacci confirmed on 6 July that [[Alessandro Nesta]] was ruled out of the final due to a groin injury he sustained against the Czech Republic on 22 June; France reported no injuries.<ref>{{cite news|last=Thomas|first=Russell|date=7 July 2006|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/07/worldcup2006.sport3|title=Injured Nesta ruled out of final in a heartbreaking hat-trick|work=The Guardian|access-date=17 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140924232855/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/07/worldcup2006.sport3|archive-date=24 September 2014|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/nesta-italy-shrug-off-favourites-tag-11899|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200724141523/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/nesta-italy-shrug-off-favourites-tag-11899|url-status=dead|archive-date=24 July 2020|title=No Nesta as Italy shrug off favourites' tag|publisher=FIFA|date=6 July 2006|access-date=6 July 2006}}</ref> Before the match started, a closing ceremony was organised by FIFA, lasting about 10 minutes and featuring [[Il Divo]] singing their song "[[The Time of Our Lives (Il Divo and Toni Braxton song)|The Time of Our Lives]]", which was the official song of the 2006 World Cup, as well as [[Shakira]] and [[Wyclef Jean]] singing a rendition of their song "[[Hips Don't Lie]]".<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2006-fifa-world-cup-closing-ceremony-programme-17195|title=2006 FIFA World Cup closing ceremony programme|publisher=FIFA|date=8 July 2006|access-date=11 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412044101/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2006-fifa-world-cup-closing-ceremony-programme-17195|archive-date=12 April 2020|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/latin/shakira-world-best-world-cup-performances-6157581/|title='La La La,' 'Waka Waka,' or 'Hips Don't Lie': Which Shakira World Cup Performance Is Best?|magazine=Billboard|date=14 July 2014|access-date=12 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412041118/https://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/latin/6157581/shakira-world-best-world-cup-performances|archive-date=12 April 2020|url-status=live}}</ref>
 
Unable to test the pitch at the Olympiastadion in Berlin, which had been softened by rain, Lippi finalised his tactical moves at the training ground of [[MSV Duisburg]], where he tested several players in multiple roles, especially [[Alessandro Del Piero]] in the unusual position of left wide midfielder.<ref name="La Repubblica 2006">{{cite news|url=
https://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/dirette/sezioni/calcio/mondiali/ottoluglio/index.html|title=Berlino, vigilia col maltempo. Lippi: 'Vince chi ha più fame'|date=8 July 2006|access-date=30 June 2026|work=La Repubblica|language=it}}</ref> He also included his assistant coach [[Ciro Ferrara]], a former Juventus defender, in the training sessions to compensate for the absence of Nesta,<ref name="Gualerzi 2006">{{cite web|last=Gualerzi|first=Valerio|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/speciale/mondiali/servizi/allenamento-vigilia-finale/allenamento-vigilia-finale/allenamento-vigilia-finale.html|title=Lippi non cambia formazione e prova rigori e fuorigioco|date=8 July 2006|access-date=10 December 2015|work=La Repubblica|language=it}}</ref> who failed to recover from the injury sustained against the Czechs,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tgcom24.mediaset.it/mondiali/articoli/articolo317586.shtml|title=Nesta salta anche la finale|date=6 July 2006|access-date=30 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151211142236/http://www.tgcom24.mediaset.it/mondiali/articoli/articolo317586.shtml|archive-date=11 December 2015|url-status=dead|website=TGcom24|language=it}}</ref> effectively handing his spot to [[Marco Materazzi]], who had performed well in the tournament up to that point. Lippi ultimately confirmed the same starting eleven that had defeated Germany in the semi-final: [[Gianluigi Buffon]] in goal, [[Fabio Cannavaro]] and Materazzi as central defenders, [[Fabio Grosso]] at left-back, [[Gianluca Zambrotta]] at right-back, [[Gennaro Gattuso]] and [[Andrea Pirlo]] as central midfielders, [[Simone Perrotta]] as the left midfielder, [[Mauro Camoranesi]] as the right midfielder, [[Francesco Totti]] as the attacking midfielder, and [[Luca Toni]] as centre forward.<ref name="Gualerzi 2006"/>

Just like the ''Azzurri'', the French starting eleven offered no surprises. Domenech persisted with a formation that was in many ways a mirror image of Lippi's team: veteran [[Fabien Barthez]] in goal—preferred from the start over [[Grégory Coupet]] despite grumbles from the press—[[William Gallas]] and [[Lilian Thuram]] as central defenders, [[Willy Sagnol]] at right-back, [[Éric Abidal]] at left-back, [[Claude Makélélé]] and [[Patrick Vieira]] as central defensive midfielders, [[Florent Malouda]] as the left winger, [[Franck Ribéry]] as the right winger, and [[Thierry Henry]] as the lone striker looking to convert finalisations from [[Zinédine Zidane]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Gualerzi|first=Valerio|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/speciale/mondiali/servizi/francia-prima-finale/francia-prima-finale/francia-prima-finale.html|title=Domenech: 'Me ne frego'. O no? E Zidane si prepara in silenzio|date=8 July 2006|access-date=30 June 2026|work=La Repubblica|language=it}}</ref> The French manager allowed the media to watch only the first fifteen minutes of the final training session, attempting to conceal his starting line-up against Italy for as long as possible.<ref name="La Repubblica 2006"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:Italy vs France 2006.jpg|thumb|left|A phase of play on a dead ball, with France's [[Lilian Thuram]] and Zidane behind, and Italy's [[Francesco Totti]] and [[Luca Toni]] on the right]]
The final started with each side scoring within the first 20&nbsp;minutes, making it the only World Cup final from [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|1990]] to [[2014 FIFA World Cup final|2014]] in which both finalists scored. Referee Elizondo awarded a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] when he deemed [[Florent Malouda]] to have been fouled by [[Marco Materazzi]].<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="Fox Soccer 2006">{{cite news|title=Cup final will be remembered for ugliness|url=http://msn.foxsports.com/soccer/story/5767794|publisher=[[Fox Soccer Channel|Fox Soccer]]|date=12 July 2006|access-date=12 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060715040434/http://msn.foxsports.com/soccer/story/5767794|archive-date=15 July 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Zinedine Zidane]] opened the scoring when he converted this kick in the 7th minute with a [[Panenka (penalty kick)|Panenka]] that glanced off the underside of the crossbar and into the goal.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/features/news/newsid=1655278/index.html|title=The cult of the Panenka penalty|publisher=FIFA|date=25 June 2012|access-date=3 July 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120702120609/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/features/news/newsid=1655278/index.html|archive-date=2 July 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Stevenson-2006" /> Materazzi redeemed himself for Italy when he levelled the score in the 19th minute, a header from an [[Andrea Pirlo]] corner.<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/italy-france-pso-25460|title=Italy 1-1 France a.e.t 5-3 PSO|publisher=FIFA|date=9 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200724143114/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/italy-france-pso-25460|archive-date=24 July 2020}}</ref> In the 36th minute, [[Luca Toni]] struck the crossbar with a header from another Pirlo corner.<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/> At half time, the score was level at 1–1. The beginning of the second half was largely controlled by France,<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/> also seeking a penalty early on when Malouda went to ground in the box after a tackle by Gianluca Zambrotta.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.football-italia.net/124228/day-italy-win-world-cup|title=On this day: Italy win the World Cup!|website=Football Italia|date=9 July 2019|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190710143347/https://www.football-italia.net/124228/day-italy-win-world-cup|archive-date=10 July 2019|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Patrick Vieira]] was replaced by [[Alou Diarra]] in the 56th minute due to an apparent hamstring injury.<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/> In the 62nd minute, Toni headed a goal that was disallowed for [[Offside (association football)|offside]] from a Grosso [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]].<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/> After the 90 minutes of regulation time, the score was still level at 1–1, forcing the match into extra time. In the 104th minute, Italian goalkeeper [[Gianluigi Buffon]] made a potentially game-saving save when he tipped a Zidane header over the crossbar that had been crossed in from [[Willy Sagnol]].<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/>

[[File:ItalyWC2006win.jpg|thumb|500px|Players of the Italy national football team lifting the World Cup trophy]]
Five minutes later, Zidane and Materazzi were jogging up the pitch alongside each other. They briefly exchanged words and Materazzi pulled at Zidane's jersey; Zidane then delivered a [[headbutt]] to Materazzi's chest, knocking him to the ground.<ref name="FIFA, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="Fox Soccer 2006"/> As the game play had switched direction, Buffon protested to the assistant referee, who did not see what had happened.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2016/07/08/zinedine-zidane-headbutt-materazzi-world-cup-france-italy-2006|title=Zinedine Zidane's World Cup final headbutt recalled, 10 years later|magazine=Sports Illustrated|date=8 July 2016|access-date=17 April 2020|archive-date=14 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210714202342/https://www.si.com/soccer/2016/07/08/zinedine-zidane-headbutt-materazzi-world-cup-france-italy-2006|url-status=live}}</ref> When the play returned and referee Elizondo saw Materazzi on the ground, he halted play to consult his assistants. According to match officials' reports, the referee and his assistants did not see what had transpired; however, Elizondo consulted fourth official [[Luis Medina Cantalejo]] via headset, who confirmed the incident.<ref name="ESPN SoccerNet 2006"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.football-italia.net/42269/ref-who-saw-zidane-hit-materazzi|title=Ref: 'Who saw Zidane hit Materazzi'|website=Football Italia|date=28 November 2013|access-date=17 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160503001620/http://www.football-italia.net/42269/ref-who-saw-zidane-hit-materazzi|archive-date=3 May 2016|url-status=live}}</ref> Elizondo then issued Zidane a [[Red card (association football)|red card]] in the 110th minute.<ref name="FIFA 2006"/><ref>{{cite news|last=Williams|first=Richard|date=10 July 2006|title=Zidane exits the stage with a walk of shame|work=The Guardian|location=London|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/10/worldcup2006.sport6|access-date=10 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060929133238/http://football.guardian.co.uk/worldcup2006/comment/story/0%2C%2C1816900%2C00.html|archive-date=29 September 2006|url-status=live}}</ref> It marked the 14th overall expulsion of Zidane's career and meant he joined [[Cameroon national football team|Cameroon]]'s [[Rigobert Song]] as the only players ever to be sent off during two separate World Cup tournaments.<ref>{{cite news|last=Buckingham|first=Mark|date=2006|title=1998 World Cup – France|publisher=Sky Sports|url=http://home.skysports.com/worldcup/historyarticle.aspx?hlid=373681|access-date=11 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060703033753/http://home.skysports.com/worldcup/historyarticle.aspx?hlid=373681|archive-date=3 July 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Pagani 2010">{{cite web|last=Pagani|first=Francesco Federico|date=22 May 2010|title=FIFA World Cup records: i record Mondiali!|url=https://sciabolatamorbida.wordpress.com/2010/05/22/fifa-world-cup-records-i-record-mondiali/|url-status=dead|website=Sciabolata Morbida|language=it|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111121120955/https://sciabolatamorbida.wordpress.com/2010/05/22/fifa-world-cup-records-i-record-mondiali/|archive-date=21 November 2011|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref>{{refn|With his sending-off in the World Cup final, Zinedine Zidane equalled the record for the most [[Yellow card (association football)|yellow cards]] (Brazil's [[Cafu]], 6) and red cards (Cameroon's Rigobert Song, 2) collected by a single player in World Cup history.<ref name="Pagani 2010"/>|group=nb}} He also became the fourth player red-carded in a World Cup final, in addition to being the first sent off in extra time.<ref>{{cite web|title=Zidane sent off in extra time for head butt|website=ESPN SoccerNet|date=9 July 2006|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373588&cc=5901|access-date=11 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060811220022/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373588&cc=5901|archive-date=11 August 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref>

After extra time, the score was still level at 1–1, forcing the match into a penalty shoot-out. France's David Trezeguet, who had scored the golden goal against Italy in the UEFA Euro 2000 final, was the only player not to score his penalty after his kick hit the crossbar, shot down after its impact, and stayed just ahead of the goal-line. [[Fabio Grosso]], who scored Italy's first goal in the semi-final win against Germany, scored the winning penalty and Italy won its fourth World Cup (its first since 1982) by a score of 5–3.<ref name="Stevenson-2006">{{cite news|last=Stevenson|first=Jonathan|date=9 July 2006|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991652.stm|title=Italy 1–1 France (aet)|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180930122300/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/4991652.stm|archive-date=30 September 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Analysis===
[[File:Italy vs France - FIFA World Cup 2006 final - Gianluigi Buffon.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Until Zidane's penalty, Italy's goalkeeper Gianluigi Buffon had kept a [[clean sheet]] for 458 minutes.]]
Following the opening performance by Shakira and Wyclef Jean, who performed a special version of "Hips Don't Lie" called "The Bamboo Version", the final commenced, with Italian President [[Giorgio Napolitano]], French President [[Jacques Chirac]], and other political figures, including the Italian sports minister Giovanna Melandri, in attendance.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> The Italian and French line-ups were exactly as anticipated on the eve of the match, captained respectively by Cannavaro—one of the two Italian starters, along with Totti, who were veterans of the Euro 2000 final,<ref name="La Repubblica, 10 July 2006">{{cite news|date=10 July 2006|title=L'Italia Campione del mondo, Gattuso: 'Ha vinto una squadra operaia'|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/dirette/sezioni/calcio/mondiali/giornofinale/index.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> and Zidane, who was playing the final match of his 19-year career. Of the 28 players who took to the pitch that night, 8 were under contract with the Italian club Juventus; in doing so, the ''Bianconeri'' equalled the record established 72 years earlier by the [[Czechoslovakia national football team|Czechoslovaks]] of [[SK Slavia Prague|Slavia Prague]].<ref>{{cite web|date=28 May 2015|title=Fußball Weltmeisterschaft 1934 in Italien|url=http://fussballweltmeisterschaft.npage.de/1934.html|website=Fußball Weltmeisterschaft|language=de|access-date=30 June 2026|url-status=dead|archive-date=28 May 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150528012348/http://fussballweltmeisterschaft.npage.de/1934.html}}</ref>

The first half was fierce and lively but above all highly physical and littered with fouls. Henry was the first to suffer from this, remaining on the ground after an accidental collision with Cannavaro just a few minutes after the opening whistle. Shortly after, the ''Azzurri'' picked up their first booking due to a foul by Zambrotta on Vieira.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> The deadlock was broken in the 7th minute when Frenchman Malouda won, with some astuteness,<ref name="Brera 2006">{{cite news|last=Mura|first=Gianni|date=10 July 2006|title=Benedetti undici metri|url=https://www.repubblica.it/sport/2020/03/21/news/benedetti_undici_metri-301024351/|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|postscript=. Republished on 21 March 2020.}}</ref> a penalty kick following a challenge from Materazzi. Zidane stepped up to the spot and attempted a [[Panenka (penalty kick)|Panenka]]-style chip, sending the ball to strike the underside of the crossbar before bouncing past the goal line, a shot against which Buffon could do nothing.<ref>{{cite book|last=Caioli|first=Luca|date=2007|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=3UdCgEvWC8EC|title=Zidane. 110 minutes pour partir|location=Paris|publisher=Éditions Prolongations|language=fr|page=51|isbn=978-2-916400-16-7|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite book|last=Lahouri|first=Besma|date=2010|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=0AZGAQAAIAAJ|title=Zidane, une vie secrète|location=Paris|publisher=Éditions Succès du livre|language=fr|page=22|isbn=978-2-08-120427-0|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref> For the first time during this edition of the World Cup, Italy found themselves trailing.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/>

The ''Azzurri'' attempted to react immediately but with little success. The Italian team played stretched out and disorganised, in contrast to a more compact and tighter French formation.<ref name="Mura 2013, p. 85">{{cite book|last=Mura|first=Gianni|date=2013|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=okTx0AEACAAJ|title=Non gioco più, me ne vado. Gregari e campioni, coppe e bidoni|location=Milan|publisher=Il Saggiatore|language=it|page=85|isbn=978-88-428-1752-9|access-date=2 July 2026}}</ref> Thuram cut out a dangerous cross from Pirlo, Totti struggled to find space, partly due to tight marking from Makélélé and Vieira,<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> and Grosso found it difficult to contain a dangerous and unpredictable Ribéry.<ref>{{cite news|last=Gualerzi|first=Valerio|date=9 July 2006|title=Buffon, Grosso, Cannavaro e Materazzi, la grande difesa ci regala la Coppa|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/speciale/mondiali/servizi/pagelle-itafra/pagelle-itafra/pagelle-itafra.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Pirlo attempted to hold the reins of an Italian game that he could not dictate as fluidly as in previous matches.<ref name="Mura 2013, p. 85"/> The equalizer came in the 19th minute, after 12 minutes of suffering, from a [[corner kick]] taken by Pirlo; Materazzi, redeeming himself for the penalty conceded earlier, outjumped Vieira and headed the ball past Barthez.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/>

The goal energised the ''Azzurri''. France was caught off guard by the aerial game of the Italian national side, which reduced their offensive impact and favored Italian possession. With the passing lanes closed off for Ribéry and Henry, the Italian pressing on Zidane was also highly effective.<ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> France reappeared near the opposing box in the 25th minute with a cross from Ribéry cut out by Materazzi. In the 36th minute, Toni came close to giving Italy the lead, striking the crossbar with a header from a corner kick taken by Pirlo. Around the half-hour mark of the first half, an electrical power failure shut down the giant screens inside the Olympiastadion and blanked the monitors in the press box, complicating the work of journalists.<ref name="La Repubblica, 10 July 2006"/> The first half, which was overall favourable to the ''Azzurri'', concluded at 1–1.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/>

[[File:Zinedine zidane wcf 2006-edit.jpg|thumb|upright|The red card received in the final was the concluding act of Zinedine Zidane's career.]]
The second half began without substitutions. The ''Azzurri'' sat back in defence, leaving room for the French to dictate play. Early on, a run by Henry culminated in a weak shot, saved comfortably by Buffon. In the 56th minute, France made their first change: Vieira came off due to injury and was replaced by [[Alou Diarra]]. Lippi reshaped his team by bringing on Iaquinta and De Rossi, the latter returning from his suspension, in place of the less impactful Totti and Perrotta respectively. A few seconds later, in the 62nd minute, a headed goal by Toni from a Grosso free kick was disallowed for [[Offside (association football)|offside]].<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> In the 79th minute, Zidane complained of severe pain in his right shoulder following a collision with Cannavaro but remained on the pitch. In the 86th minute, Lippi substituted Del Piero for a tired Camoranesi. After two minutes of stoppage time, with the score deadlocked at 1–1, the match headed into extra time.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/>

In extra time, the teams maintained the same tactics. France engineered two highly dangerous opportunities: a shot by Ribéry in the 99th minute that flashed just wide and a powerful header from Zidane in the 104th minute that was tipped over the bar by a reflex save from Buffon, who tipped the ball over the crossbar. Domenech adjusted his formation, shifting Henry out wide and deploying Trezeguet, who had just replaced Ribéry, as a central striker. During the brief interval, an exhausted Henry left the pitch for [[Sylvain Wiltord]]. A few minutes later, Zidane and Materazzi were walking back toward the French half when a verbal exchange took place between the two. Just as everything appeared to have returned to normal, after taking a few steps forward, Zidane suddenly turned around and delivered a headbutt to Materazzi's chest, sending him falling to the ground.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/> The foul, flagged to referee Elizondo by the fourth official Cantalejo,<ref>{{cite news|date=7 March 2016|title=L'arbitro Elizondo: 'Non vidi testata di Zidane'|url=http://www.agi.it/sport/calcio/notizie/201005291501-spr-rom0054-mondiali_l_arbitro_elizondo_non_vidi_testata_di_zidane|agency=AGI|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|url-status=dead|archive-date=7 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160307043149/http://www.agi.it/sport/calcio/notizie/201005291501-spr-rom0054-mondiali_l_arbitro_elizondo_non_vidi_testata_di_zidane}}</ref> resulted in a straight red card for Zidane;<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/><ref name="Pagani 2010"/> however, Italy was unable to capitalise on their numerical superiority, and extra time ended in a stalemate. For the second time in history, following the 1994 final (in which Italy was also a finalist), the World Cup had to be decided by a penalty shoot-out.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/>

After four penalties each, the ''Azzurri'' had all converted successfully through Pirlo, Materazzi, De Rossi, and Del Piero, while France had scored through Wiltord, Abidal, and Sagnol but missed their second attempt through Trezeguet, whose shot struck the crossbar. It was Grosso who converted the definitive spot-kick to seal a 5–3 victory, delivering Italy the fourth World Cup title in their history after those of 1934, 1938, and 1982.<ref name="La Repubblica, 9 July 2006"/><ref name="De Stefano 2006"/><ref>{{cite web|date=9 July 2016|title=Dieci anni fa Italia campione del mondo. L'anniversario su Twitter: 'Indimenticabile'|url=https://www.repubblica.it/sport/2016/07/09/foto/italia_francia_campioni_del_mondo_rigori_ricorrenza-143735798/|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> At the conclusion of the match, Italian midfielder [[Andrea Pirlo]] was named Man of the Match; he had been previously named the Man of the Match against Ghana in the group stage and against Germany in the semi-final.<ref name="Man of the Match">{{cite web|date=9 July 2006|title=Budweiser Man of the Match: Andrea PIRLO (ITA)|url=http://fifaworldcup.yahoo.com/06/en/060709/1/8qgg.html|publisher=FIFA|access-date=9 July 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060719220634/http://fifaworldcup.yahoo.com/06/en/060709/1/8qgg.html|archive-date=19 July 2006}}</ref> Overall, France performed well (Zidane was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Ball]] as the best player of the tournament),<ref>{{cite AV media|date=5 July 2020|title=Zinedine Zidane's headbutt on Marco Materazzi {{!}} Germany 2006 {{!}} FIFA World Cup|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jb16JvUCVQs|type=video|publisher=FIFA|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|via=YouTube}}</ref> occupying the pitch effectively and maintaining width.<ref name="Mura 2013, p. 85"/> On the other hand, Italy won the World Cup through highly organised collective play and excellent physical conditioning; in particular, Buffon concluded the tournament without conceding a single goal from open play by an opponent (the only one he conceded was caused by teammate [[Cristian Zaccardo]] via an [[own goal]] in the match against the United States), winning the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Glove]] as the best goalkeeper of the tournament, while centre-back Cannavaro (who went on to win the 2006 Ballon d'Or, with Buffon as runner-up) received the [[FIFA World Cup Silver Ball]] as the second best player of the tournament—a testament to the quality of the ''Azzurri'' defence, which was decisively their strongest department throughout the entire competition.<ref name="Mura 2013, p. 85"/>

