===Viewership===
According to FIFA, 715.1&nbsp;million individuals globally watched the final match of this tournament.<ref>{{cite web|date=2009|url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/marketing/factsfigures/tvdata.html|title=TV Data|publisher=FIFA|access-date=24 November 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090830065721/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/marketing/factsfigures/tvdata.html|archive-date=30 August 2009|quote=The final Italy – France [had] a global cumulative audience of 715.1 million viewers.|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Interpublic Group of Companies|IPG]]'s independent media agency Initiative Worldwide estimated a 260&nbsp;million people viewership.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601077&sid=aOYdMOmxoV4c&refer=intsports|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090209011218/http://bloomberg.com/apps/news?pid=20601077&sid=aOYdMOmxoV4c&refer=intsports|url-status=dead|archive-date=9 February 2009|title=Soccer World Cup Final Had Sport's Largest TV Audience of 2006|publisher=Bloomberg|date=19 December 2006}}</ref> The independent firm Initiative Futures Sport + Entertainment estimates it at 322&nbsp;million viewers.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.sportingintelligence.com/2010/06/10/world-cup-final-will-vie-for-record-of-second-most-watched-event-in-human-history-100605/|title=World Cup final 'will vie for record of second most-watched event in human history'|website=Sporting Intelligence|date=10 June 2010|access-date=9 January 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110501033521/http://www.sportingintelligence.com/2010/06/10/world-cup-final-will-vie-for-record-of-second-most-watched-event-in-human-history-100605/|archive-date=1 May 2011|url-status=live}}</ref> As for the viewership in the two countries involved, the broadcast on Italy's [[Rai 1]] reached a maximum of 25,384,000 viewers and an 87.03% share during the penalty shoot-out, while the broadcast on France's [[TF1]] had a general average of 22,140,000 viewers and an 80.03% share. This is the highest audience figure recorded by Italy's Auditel in the 2000s, while for France this is the second highest audience figure since 1989, the year in which [[Médiamétrie]] began its surveys.<ref>{{cite news|title=Italia-Francia, in 28 milioni davanti alla tv|url=https://www.corriere.it/Speciali/Extra/2006/Germania2006/diario/Auditelfinale.html|work=Corriere della Sera|date=10 July 2006|access-date=30 June 2026|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Classifica Auditel: i 50 programmi più visti degli ultimi 10 anni (2001-2011). Al primo posto Italia-Francia 2006|url=https://www.tvblog.it/post/auditel-programmi-piu-visti-classifica-decennale|website=TvBlog|date=4 April 2012|access-date=30 June 2026|language=it}}</ref>

==Post-match==
===Celebrations and aftermath===
[[File:Inchada en Roma.JPG|thumb|left|Italian fans celebrating the 2006 World Cup win at the [[Circus Maximus]] in [[Rome]]]]
German president [[Horst Köhler]], UEFA president [[Lennart Johansson]], and the local organising committee president [[Franz Beckenbauer]] were among those present on the pitch stage during the awards ceremony. President Köhler handed the trophy to Italian captain Fabio Cannavaro without FIFA president Sepp Blatter's presence.<ref name="FIFA 2018">{{cite AV media|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nELaL14ms7A|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211114/nELaL14ms7A|archive-date=14 November 2021|url-status=live|title=Matchday Live – 2006 Italy vs. France|publisher=FIFA|date=4 May 2018|type=video|access-date=4 May 2018|via=YouTube}}{{cbignore}}</ref> As Cannavaro raised the trophy, a short version of [[Patrizio Buanne]]'s "Stand Up (Champions Theme)" was played.<ref name="FIFA 2018"/> The victory also led to Italy topping the [[FIFA World Rankings]] in February 2007 for the first time since November 1993.<ref>{{cite web|title=Italy oust Brazil to take top spot|url=https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/news/y=2007/m=2/news=italy-oust-brazil-take-top-spot-111685.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202143215/http://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/news/y=2007/m=2/news=italy-oust-brazil-take-top-spot-111685.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=2 February 2017|publisher=FIFA|date=14 February 2007|access-date=14 February 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.squawka.com/en/news/every-major-nations-lowest-fifa-rank-since-records-began/1058227#gFyku6VSM88a4spv.97|title=Every major nation's lowest FIFA rank since records began|website=Squawka|date=10 September 2018|access-date=12 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181215215937/http://www.squawka.com/en/news/every-major-nations-lowest-fifa-rank-since-records-began/1058227#gFyku6VSM88a4spv.97|archive-date=15 December 2018|url-status=live}}</ref> Pirlo was awarded the Man of the Match.<ref name="Man of the Match"/> The day after the final, Zidane was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Ball]] as the player of the tournament.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2006-fifa-world-cup-official-fifa-awards-zidane-wins-adidas-golden-bal-16415|title=2006 FIFA World Cup Official FIFA Awards: Zidane wins adidas Golden Ball award|publisher=FIFA|date=10 July 2006|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180714151444/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/2006-fifa-world-cup-official-fifa-awards-zidane-wins-adidas-golden-bal-16415|archive-date=14 July 2018|url-status=live}}</ref>

For the front page of ''[[Corriere della Sera]]'', Italy's newspaper of record, [[Gianni Riotta]] wrote: "In twenty-five years' time, when the generation of boys and girls who are still sleeping blissfully after the night of celebration for Italy's world championship grows up, the memories will remain. In the scrapbook of a lifetime's souvenirs, 9 July 2006 will be pure joy: 'When Italy won, I...{{'"}}<ref>{{cite web|last=Bertocco|first=Fabrizio|date=9 July 2020|title=9 luglio 2006: eravamo Campioni del Mondo per la quarta volta|url=https://sport.periodicodaily.com/9-luglio-2006-eravamo-campioni-del-mondo-per-la-quarta-volta/|website=Periodico Daily Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Leading sports journalist [[Gianni Brera]] described it as a "historic win" for Italy.<ref name="Brera 2006"/>

The Italy team celebrated its victory with a parade in Rome the day after the final on 10 July.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/5163914.stm|title=Italian joy at World Cup victory|date=10 July 2006|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131104142416/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/5163914.stm|archive-date=4 November 2013|url-status=live}}</ref> It was attended by 500,000 people as the team traveled to the [[Circus Maximus]]. The team also met with President Giorgio Napolitano and Prime Minister [[Romano Prodi]], where all members of the World Cup-winning squad were awarded the [[Order of Merit of the Italian Republic]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/italy/5164410.stm|title=Italy squad given heroes' welcome|date=10 July 2006|work=BBC Sport|access-date=18 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131104142452/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/italy/5164410.stm|archive-date=4 November 2013|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=The eight lesser-known football World Cup heroes|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2022/11/18/lesser-known-world-cup-heroes|agency=Al Jazeera|date=18 November 2022|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> On 11 July, the squad delivered the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy]] to the former Juventus player [[Gianluca Pessotto]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Mauro|first=Alberto|date=11 July 2006|title=La Coppa da Pessotto|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Squadre/Juventus/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/11/cannavaro-pessotto.shtml|access-date=25 June 2026|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it}}</ref> who was still in hospital after he fell from a window in Turin in what was alleged to have been a suicide attempt,<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/> and dedicated the win to him.<ref>{{cite web|last=Menicucci|first=Paolo|date=11 July 2006|title=Azzurri return home in triumph|url=https://it.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/0254-0d7b8c0cff65-4d1a6c5b033f-1000--azzurri-return-home-in-triumph/|access-date=25 June 2026|website=UEFA.com}}</ref>

Italy manager Marcello Lippi said after the game that the win was "an immense joy" and that it took "a lot of determination and a lot of conviction in penalty shoot-outs {{endash}} it's not by chance. Our boys really wanted it and that's why they were able to put away all five", and defended Marco Materazzi while France manager Raymond Domenech defended Zinedine Zidane and lamented the loss, saying Materazzi was "the man of the match, not Andrea Pirlo", and that he was "deeply disappointed" as he felt they "deserved the title based over the whole match".<ref>{{cite news|title=Lippi: 'Una gioia immensa'|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/09/lippi.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|date=9 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026|language=it}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Domenech laments loss|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2006/7/9/domenech-laments-loss|agency=Al Jazeera|date=9 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Reaction to Zidane's sending off|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/france/5152728.stm|work=BBC Sport|date=10 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> After winning the World Cup, Lippi also stated that this was his "most satisfying moment as a coach", even after winning the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]] and the UEFA Champions League with Juventus.<ref>{{cite news|last=Evans|first=Simon|date=10 July 2006|url=http://www.tiscali.co.uk/news/newswire.php/news/reuters/2006/07/10/sport/my-most-satisfying-moment-lippi.html|title=My most satisfying moment – Lippi|access-date=28 June 2026|agency=Reuters|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090204184842/http://www.tiscali.co.uk/news/newswire.php/news/reuters/2006/07/10/sport/my-most-satisfying-moment-lippi.html|archive-date=4 February 2009|via=Tiscali News}}</ref> Three days after the final, Lippi did not renew his expiring contract with the FIGC and left his office as manager of Italy, citing insults to himself and his son.<ref>{{cite news|last=Licari|first=Fabio|date=12 July 2006|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/12/lippilascia.shtml|title=Lippi dice basta. Ecco perché|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> He was succeeded by [[Roberto Donadoni]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/sezioni/sport/calcio/donadoni-nuovo-ct/donadoni-nuovo-ct/donadoni-nuovo-ct.html|title=Nazionale, scelto l'erede di Lippi Donadoni è il nuovo ct degli azzurri|language=it|work=La Repubblica|date=13 July 2006|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref>

On 14 July, five days after the final, the FIGC's Federal Commission of Appeal (despite this name, it was controversially used as a first-instance court), led by [[Cesare Ruperto]],<ref name="Arpino 2018"/> issued the first ''Calciopoli'' sentence that relegated Fiorentina, Lazio, and Juventus to Serie B.<ref>{{cite AV media|last1=Tesone|first1=Luca|last2=Rinelli|first2=Fabrizio|date=12 December 2018|title=9 luglio 2006, Italia Campione del Mondo: retroscena e momenti chiave di un Mondiale indimenticabile|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-v0KS8AxnK4|type=video|website=Fanpage.it|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|via=YouTube}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=6 January 2019|title=Storie di calcio 2005-2006: 91 punti cancellati da Calciopoli|url=https://radioblackandwhite1897.wordpress.com/2019/01/06/storie-di-calcio-2005-2006-91-punti-cancellati-da-calciopoli/|website=Radio Black and White 1897|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Garbo|first=Daniele|date=8 July 2020|title=16 anni dopo: era tutto vero. Campioni del mondo!|url=https://www.allonsanfan.it/2020/07/08/mondiale-2006-italia-campione/|website=Allonsanfàn|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> On 25 July, the FIGC's Federal Court (acting as the appeal court), led by Piero Sandulli, saved all clubs but Juventus from [[relegation]], explicitily and controversially citing "people's feelings" in the sentence,<ref name="Arpino 2018"/> claiming that is what the people wanted.<ref name="Cambiaghi & Dent 2007"/><ref name="Zampini 2023"/><ref name="Longhi & Molinari 2023"/> Italy and France met again in the [[UEFA Euro 2008 qualifying]], where the ''Azzurri'' had to concede a 3–1 defeat in Paris before drawing 0–0 in Milan. Before ultimately losing out in the quarter-finals to eventual winners Spain in a penalty shoot-out, Italy beat France 2–0 in the group stage in what was their first win against France without extra time since their victory (2–1) during the group stage of the [[1978 World Cup]] in Argentina.<ref name="Today.it 2012"/>

===Blatter controversy===
FIFA president Sepp Blatter generated significant controversy by breaking from tradition and refusing to attend the post-match trophy ceremony.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/sport/archives/2006/07/12/2003318491|title=World Cup: Press mystified by Blatter's absence at medal ceremony|work=Taipei Times|date=12 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="Blatter-CBC">{{cite news|url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/blatter-apologizes-to-italy-over-world-cup-snub-1.692402|title=Blatter apologizes to Italy over World Cup snub|publisher=CBC Sports|date=8 January 2007|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://sport.sky.it/calcio/serie-a/2017/10/04/blatter-intervista-l-italia-non-mi-ama|title=Blatter: 'L'Italia non mi ama? Perché non la premiai nel 2006. Ho sbagliato'|work=Sky Sport|date=4 October 2017|language=it|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> Instead of personally presenting the World Cup trophy to the victorious Italy national football team, Blatter left the pitch before the presentation.<ref name="Blatter-CBC" /> Blatter's absence from the crowning of the world champions drew heavy criticism from Italian sports figures and media outlets.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.smh.com.au/sport/soccer/blatter-misquoted-over-italy-world-cup-apology-20061031-gdopzi.html|title=Blatter 'misquoted' over Italy World Cup apology|work=The Sydney Morning Herald|date=31 October 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> Blatter gave various versions to explain his reasons.<ref>{{cite news|date=8 January 2007|title=Blatter: 'Ecco perché non ho premiato l'Italia'|url=https://www.corriere.it/Primo_Piano/Sport/2007/01_Gennaio/08/blatter.html|work=Corriere della Sera|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Celeste|first=Emanuele|date=8 June 2013|title=Blatter esce allo scoperto: 'Ecco perché non premiai l'Italia campione del mondo', ma si contraddice|url=https://www.soccermagazine.it/nazionali/blatter-esce-allo-scoperto-ecco-perche-non-premiai-litalia-campione-del-mondo-ma-si-contraddice-121745/|work=Soccer Magazine|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=5 June 2015|title=Blatter contro il resto del mondo: l'attacco all'Italia e i Mondiali comprati dalla Germania|url=https://www.sportreview.it/storie-di-calcio/blatter-contro-resto-mondo-italia|website=SportReview|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> To justify this decision, he initially explained that he had decided to let European confederation officials present the awards to the two finalists since both national teams were from Europe; he later changed his version, claiming he made the choice because the entire stadium had booed and insulted him on the opening day of the tournament (9 June in [[Munich]]).<ref>{{cite news|date=12 July 2006|title=Parla il presidente Blatter: 'Fifa, Zidane rischia il premio'|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/speciale/mondiali/servizi/parla-blatter/parla-blatter/parla-blatter.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

As eight Juventus players were involved in the final and as he grew significantly under scrutiny for his criticism and statements about Italy, Blatter explained that the reason he did not attend Italy's celebration ceremony had nothing to do with ''Calciopoli'' and elaborated, "I have spoken well of Italy: they deserved the World Cup, the players reacted brilliantly to demonstrate that 'Calciopoli' involved the directors and not them. This is definitive."<ref>{{cite news|last=Licari|first=Fabio|date=3 November 2006|title=Blatter: 'Non ce l'ho con l'Italia'|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Altro_Calcio/Primo_Piano/2006/11_Novembre/03/blatter.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Nonetheless, Juventus supporters in particular critisised him, citing his close friendship with Walter Gagg (head of the FIFA Stadiums Committee), who was in turn close with the then Inter Milan chairman [[Giacinto Facchetti]] (as discovered in the ''Calciopoli'' wiretaps),<ref>{{cite web|date=25 May 2010|title=L'informativa che non c'è|url=https://download.ju29ro.com/files/L'informativa_che_non_c'e'.pdf|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> as well as his December 2007 statements to Italy's leading news agency [[Agenzia Nazionale Stampa Associata|ANSA]]. Blatter praised and personally thanked [[Luca Cordero di Montezemolo]], who had ties to the then Inter Milan owner [[Marco Tronchetti Provera]],<ref>{{cite web|date=9 February 2007|title=Io do una cosa a te, tu ne dai una a me?|url=http://blog.ju29ro.com/2007/11/io-do-una-cosa-te-tu-ne-dai-una-me.html|website=Ju29ro.com|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|postscript=. Citing as sources the 9 February 2007 edition of ''[[Il Sole 24 Ore]]'' and an 8 November 2007 article by ANSA.}}</ref> for his role as mediator when the scandal broke out, having convinced Juventus (which suffered threats that Italy be excluded from international competitions) to not turn to the ordinary courts after the sanctions resulting from the scandal, which could have cleared the club's name but at the cost of Italy having to miss the World Cup or causing other issues to Italian football, which would have had to postpone the subsequent league season.<ref>{{cite web|date=5 December 2007|title=Blatter incastra Montezemolo|url=http://blog.ju29ro.com/2007/12/blatter-incastra-montezemolo.html|website=Ju29ro Blog|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=17 February 2018|title=Penta: la mia Calciopoli (parte terza)|url=https://ilcalcioeugualepertutti.wordpress.com/2018/02/17/penta-la-mia-calciopoli-parte-terza/|website=Il calcio è uguale per tutti|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Moreover, Blatter found himself at the centre of further controversy following comments against the ''Azzurri'', who he claimed were undeserving of the world title because they had qualified for the quarter-finals only due to what he deemed a generous penalty against Australia.<ref>{{cite news|last=Mangini|first=Claudio|date=8 June 2014|title=2006: quello slalom di Grosso abbattuto in area, e l'Italia decollò|url=http://www.ilsecoloxix.it/p/sport/2014/06/08/ARrN5Yd-abbattuto_decollo_grosso.shtml|work=Il Secolo XIX|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180110234038/http://www.ilsecoloxix.it/p/sport/2014/06/08/ARrN5Yd-abbattuto_decollo_grosso.shtml|archive-date=10 January 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Miller|first=Nick|date=5 December 2014|title=Golden goal: Fabio Grosso for Italy v Germany (2006)|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/dec/05/sp-golden-goal-fabio-grosso-italy-germany-2006|work=The Guardian|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> [[Gigi Riva]], Italy's former player and the then FIGC deputy commissioner, had called on Blatter to undergo a [[CT scan]] and respect Italy.<ref>{{cite news|date=29 October 2006|title=Blatter contro la Nazionale|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Nazionale/Primo_Piano/2006/10_Ottobre/29/blatter.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Mazzone|first=Elisa|date=23 December 2011|title=Blatter: 'L'Italia campione del mondo fu un errore'|url=https://www.ilgiornale.it/news/blatter-italia-campione-mondo-errore.html|work=Il Giornale|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Shortly afterward, Blatter backed down and apologised for his remarks.<ref>{{cite news|date=30 October 2006|title=Italia mondiale, Blatter fa marcia indietro e Fiorello lo perseguita sul cellulare|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/10/sezioni/sport/calcio/blatter-mondiali/blatter-mondiali/blatter-mondiali.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

In January 2007, Blatter made an official visit to Rome and issued a formal apology to the FIGC.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Nazionale/Primo_Piano/2007/01_Gennaio/08/blatter.shtml|title=Blatter: 'Mancata premiazione? Un errore'|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|date=8 January 2007|language=it|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://timesofmalta.com/article/blatter-apologises-to-italy-for-world-cup-snub.30213|title=Blatter apologises to Italy for World Cup snub|work=Times of Malta|date=8 January 2007|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> He admitted that his absence in Berlin was "an error", explaining that he had initially planned to skip the presentation beforehand to avoid an "ugly scene" and public jeering from the German crowd toward FIFA.<ref name="Blatter-CBC" /> In September 2008, Blatter finally delivered the trophy to Cannavaro upon the latter's request, as well as the FIFA badge.<ref>{{cite news|date=2 September 2008|title=Pace fatta tra Blatter e l'Italia: consegna la Coppa a Cannavaro|url=https://sport.sky.it/calcio/2008/09/02/blatter_consegna_coppa_a_cannavaro|work=Sky Sport Italia|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> In 2013, Blatter described the tense aftermath in Germany as a personal "hell", maintaining that his decision was solely intended to shelter the award ceremony from political whistling.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.corriere.it/sport/13_giugno_07/germania-2006-blatter-non-premiai-italia-campione-mondo-inferno_bdc27996-cf93-11e2-b6a8-ee7758ca2279.shtml|title=Germania 2006, Blatter: «Non premiai l'Italia campione del mondo. Fu un inferno»|work=Corriere della Sera|date=7 June 2013|language=it|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> In July 2012, Blatter had also alleged that the assignation of the 2006 World Cup to Germany had been bought.<ref>{{cite news|last=Vendemiale|first=Lorenzo|date=16 July 2012|title=Blatter: 'Assegnazione dei Mondiali 2006 alla Germania fu comprata'|url=https://www.ilfattoquotidiano.it/2012/07/16/blatter-assegnazione-dei-mondiali-2006-alla-germania-fu-comprata/295782/|work=Il Fatto Quotidiano|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

==Zidane–Materazzi incident==
===FIFA sanctions===
[[File:Zidaneheadbutt.jpg|thumb|500px|A sequence showing Zinedine Zidane headbutting [[Marco Materazzi]] and being sent off]]
Zidane's headbutt on Materazzi during extra time sparked controversy and a major media impact.<ref>{{cite book|last=Toussaint|first=Jean-Philippe|date=<!-- August -->2006|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=UhIsAQAAIAAJ|title=La mélancolie de Zidane|location=Paris|publisher=Les Éditions de Minuit|language=fr|page=5|isbn=978-2-7073-1999-9|access-date=2 July 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref><ref name="Baetens 2006">{{cite journal|last=Baetens|first=Franck|date=November 2006|url=https://shs.cairn.info/revue-esprit-2006-11-page-127|title=Le coup de tête de Zidane: Pour une lecture mythologique|journal=Esprit|issue=11|pages=127–147|doi=10.3917/espri.0611.0127|access-date=2 July 2026|via=Cairn.info}}</ref><ref name="Manghi 2007, p. 5">{{cite book|last=Manghi|first=Sergio|date=2007|url=https://books.google.it/books/about/Zidane_Anatomia_di_una_testata_mondiale.html?id=54KIGQAACAAJ|title=Zidane. Anatomia di una testata mondiale|location=Troina|publisher=Città Aperta|language=it|page=5|isbn=978-88-8137-287-4|access-date=2 July 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref> On 11 July, [[FIFA]] opened disciplinary proceedings to investigate the incident and also affirmed the legality of Elizondo's decision to send Zidane off, rejecting claims that Cantalejo had illegally relied on video transmission to make a decision about handling Zidane's misconduct.<ref>{{cite press release|date=11 July 2006|title=FIFA to review dramatic World Cup final|publisher=FIFA|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany2006/media/newsid=13829.html|access-date=11 June 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100614214137/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/germany2006/media/newsid%3D13829.html|archive-date=14 June 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> Regarding the incident, after conducting an investigation and confirming the absence of [[racial]] slurs, FIFA issued a CHF 5,000 fine and a two-match ban against Materazzi, while Zidane received a three-match ban and a CHF 7,500 fine,<ref>{{cite news|date=20 July 2006|title=Sentenza FIFA sul caso Zidane: tre turni al francese, due a Materazzi|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2006/07/sezioni/sport/calcio/sentenza-fifa/sentenza-fifa/sentenza-fifa.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> 
respectively equivalent to €3,183 and €4,774.<ref>{{cite news|date=20 July 2006|title=La Fifa punisce Zidane e anche Materazzi|url=https://www.corriere.it/Primo_Piano/Sport/2006/07_Luglio/20/zidane.shtml|work=Corriere della Sera|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Since Zidane had already retired, he voluntarily served three days of community service on FIFA's behalf as a substitute for the match ban.<ref>{{cite news|last=Bose|first=Mihir|date=21 July 2006|title=Zidane case sets disciplinary precedent|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/international/2341128/Zidane-case-sets-disciplinary-precedent.html|url-status=live|access-date=7 December 2013|work=The Daily Telegraph|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251109153123/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/international/2341128/Zidane-case-sets-disciplinary-precedent.html|archive-date=9 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Hughes|first=Rob|date=21 July 2006|title=Suspensions in World Cup Head-Butting Incident|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/07/21/sports/soccer/21soccer.html|url-status=live|access-date=21 June 2024|work=The New York Times|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250828034848/https://www.nytimes.com/2006/07/21/sports/soccer/21soccer.html|archive-date=28 August 2025}}</ref>

===Zidane and Materazzi's views===
On 12 July, in an interview broadcast on French television, Zidane publicly apologised for his violent act and revealed that his reaction was not triggered by racist abuse but rather by severe and repeated insults directed by the Italian defender toward his family.<ref name="BBC Sport 2006">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/france/5174758.stm|title=Zidane explains|work=BBC Sport|date=12 July 2006|access-date=2 February 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070222213506/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2006/teams/france/5174758.stm|archive-date=22 February 2007|url-status=live}}</ref> Zidane only partly explained that repeated harsh [[maternal insult]]s had caused him to react.<ref>{{cite news|last=Hughes|first=Matt|date=11 July 2006|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/crime/article/read-my-lips-the-taunt-that-made-zidane-snap-nrkvdh8zfkx|title=Read my lips: the taunt that made Zidane snap|work=[[The Times]]|location=London|access-date=11 July 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140115070510/http://www.thetimes.co.uk/tto/sport/football/international/article1747603.ece|archive-date=15 January 2014|url-status=live}}</ref> Materazzi admitted [[trash talking]] Zidane, but argued that Zidane's behaviour had been very arrogant and that the remarks were trivial.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373706&cc=5901|title=Materazzi admits to insulting Zidane|website=ESPN SoccerNet|date=11 July 2006|access-date=2 February 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140115071331/http://espnfc.com/news/story?id=373706&cc=5901|archive-date=15 January 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> Materazzi also insisted that he did not insult Zidane's mother (who was ill at the time), saying, "I didn't talk about his mother, either. I lost my mother when I was fifteen, and even now I still get emotional talking about it."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=373767&cc=5901|title=Zidane: Materazzi insulted my family|website=ESPN SoccerNet|date=12 July 2006|access-date=2 February 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140115072215/http://espnfc.com/news/story?id=373767&cc=5901|archive-date=15 January 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=McCourt|first=Ian|date=25 March 2018|title=World Cup stunning moments: Zinedine Zidane's head-butt|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/mar/11/world-cup-moments-zinedine-zidane-head-butt|work=The Guardian|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

[[File:Marco Materazzi.jpg|thumb|[[Marco Materazzi]] in July 2006]]
In his first interview, Zidane said that he did not regret his actions because "it would be like admitting that he was right to say all that".<ref name="BBC Sport 2006"/> According to the [[BBC]], Materazzi said, "I wish an ugly death to you and all your family", and then told Zidane to "go fuck yourself". [[SOS Racisme]], a Paris-based anti-racism group, said that "several very well informed sources" had suggested Materazzi called Zidane a "dirty terrorist".<ref>{{cite news|date=11 July 2006|title=Lip-reader reveals what riled Zidane|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/11/worldcup2006.sport6|work=The Guardian|access-date=1 July 2026}}</ref> Materazzi offered his version of events two months later, stating that after he had grabbed Zidane's jersey, Zidane remarked, "If you want my shirt, I will give it to you afterwards", and he replied to Zidane that he would prefer his sister, but said during the interview that he was unaware Zidane even had a sister.<ref>{{cite news|title=Materazzi reveals details of Zidane World Cup slur|agency=Reuters|date=5 September 2006|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=378084&cc=5901|access-date=2 February 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090405025125/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=378084&cc=5901|archive-date=5 April 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=6 September 2006|title=Materazzi on Zidane's 2006 World Cup headbutt: 'I mentioned his sister'|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37479167/mentioned-sister|website=ESPN on Soccer|access-date=1 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Fifield|first=Dominic|date=6 September 2006|title=Materazzi reveals what he said to Zidane on eve of final rerun|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/sep/06/newsstory.sport7|work=The Guardian|access-date=1 July 2026}}</ref>

After video evidence suggested that Materazzi had verbally provoked Zidane into the headbutt, several British [[Tabloid journalism|tabloids]], such as the ''[[Daily Mail]]'',<ref>{{cite news|last=Brook|first=Stephen|date=3 November 2008|title=Mail pays libel damages over racism claims|url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2008/nov/04/daily-mail-materazzi-racism-libel|work=The Guardian|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Phillips|first=Jane|date=4 November 2008|title=Materazzi accepts damages from British newspaper|url=https://www.brecorder.com/news/3569496/materazzi-accepts-damages-from-british-newspaper-20081104830770|work=Business Recorder|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> the ''[[Daily Star (United Kingdom)|Daily Star]]'',<ref>{{cite news|date=3 April 2008|title=Materazzi wins apology over Zidane headbutt claims|url=https://www.si.com/soccer/2008/04/03/materazzi-apology|work=Sports Illustrated|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=8 April 2008|title='Daily Star' settles suit with Marco Materazzi|url=https://www.taipeitimes.com/News/sport/archives/2008/04/08/2003408653|work=Taipei Times|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> and ''[[The Sun (United Kingdom)|The Sun]]'',<ref>{{cite news|last=Heffer|first=Steven|date=5 February 2009|title=Materazzi wins damages against UK paper|url=https://www.abc.net.au/news/2009-02-06/materazzi-wins-damages-against-uk-paper/285496|work=ABC News|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref> claimed to have hired [[lip readers]] to determine what Materazzi had said, with all three newspapers alleging that Materazzi called Zidane "the son of a terrorist whore".<ref>{{cite news|date=16 March 2008|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7281881.stm|title=Materazzi wins Daily Star apology|work=BBC News|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090112083757/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/uk_news/7281881.stm|archive-date=12 January 2009|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=9 June 2008|title=Sun apologises to Marco Materazzi over 'racist' slur|work=Press Gazette|access-date=21 January 2020|url=https://www.pressgazette.co.uk/sun-apologises-to-marco-materazzi-over-racist-slur/|archive-date=14 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210714202324/https://www.pressgazette.co.uk/sun-apologises-to-marco-materazzi-over-racist-slur/|url-status=live}}</ref> Materazzi disputed this claim, eventually winning public apologies, statements read in open court, and libel damages from all three newspapers in 2008 and 2009.<ref>{{cite news|last=Gibb|first=Frances|date=25 November 2008|title=In the city: How to say sorry|url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/law/article/in-the-city-how-to-say-sorry-d6cz7xf37p3|work=The Times|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Sharrock|first=David|date=6 February 2009|title=Materazzi wins British libel damages over Sun's claims about Zidane head-butt|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/feb/06/marco-materazzi-zinedine-zidane-the-sun-libel|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110313163651/http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2009/feb/06/marco-materazzi-zinedine-zidane-the-sun-libel|archive-date=13 March 2011|work=The Guardian|access-date=9 February 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=6 February 2009|title=Materazzi wins libel damages against British tabloid|url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/materazzi-wins-libel-damages-against-british-tabloid-1.851208|work=CBC Sports|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref>

In late 2006, Materazzi released a book titled ''What I Really Told Zidane'', which contained 250 versions of what he had said to Zidane, including three curses from the ''[[Harry Potter]]'' series,<ref>{{cite web|date=9 July 2019|title=One Shot: Zidane's famous headbutt in 2006 World Cup final|url=https://sport.mediamax.am/en/news/one-shot/34149|website=Mediamax Sport|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> as well as "Zidane, what are you doing? You haven't lost yet and already you've ripped your hair out", "Since Foucault died, French philosophy has sucked", and "Where exactly is the sternum?"<ref>{{cite news|date=25 October 2006|title=Materazzi's book: 'What I really told Zidane'|work=Soccer Leagues}}</ref> In August 2007, Materazzi stated that he had insulted the French playmaker's sister, prompting Zidane's violent reaction.<ref>{{cite news|date=18 August 2007|title=Materazzi-Zidane, mistero svelato|url=http://www.repubblica.it/2007/08/sezioni/sport/calcio/frase-materazzi/frase-materazzi/frase-materazzi.html|work=La Repubblica|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Over a year after the incident, Materazzi also confirmed that his precise words to Zidane were: "I prefer the whore that is your sister."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2007/aug/18/newsstory.sport2|title=And Materazzi's exact words to Zidane were...|work=The Guardian|location=London|date=18 August 2007|access-date=18 March 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070821090226/http://football.guardian.co.uk/News_Story/0%2C%2C2151641%2C00.html|archive-date=21 August 2007|url-status=live}}</ref> In his 2021 memoirs, it was rendered in English as "I'd prefer you brought me your sister, that bitch."<ref>{{cite web|date=16 August 2021|title=Materazzi reveals what he really told Zidane|url=https://www.unisport.dk/stories/2090-materazzi-reveals-what-he-really-told-zidane/|website=Unisport|access-date=1 July 2026}}</ref>

In October 2009, in an interview conducted on French radio station [[RTL (French radio)|RTL]], Zidane stated: "Let's not forget that provocation is a terrible thing. I have never been one to provoke; I have never done it. It's terrible, and it is best not to react."<ref>{{cite news|title=Zidane : 'C'était trop fort'|work=L'Équipe|language=fr|date=12 October 2009|url=https://www.lequipe.fr/Football/breves2009/20091012_220259_zidane-2006-c-etait-trop-fort.html|access-date=12 October 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091013110223/http://www.lequipe.fr/Football/breves2009/20091012_220259_zidane-2006-c-etait-trop-fort.html|archive-date=13 October 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref> In a later interview with ''[[Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]]'' magazine, he said, "If you look at the 14 red cards I had in my career, 12 of them were a result of provocation. This isn't justification, this isn't an excuse, but my passion, temper and blood made me react."<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Pomroy|first=Matt|date=6 July 2014|title=Zinedine Zidane|url=http://www.esquireme.com/zinedine-zidane/<!-- https://www.esquireme.com/culture/zinedine-zidane -->|magazine=Esquire Middle East|access-date=16 April 2015|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150416133836/http://www.esquireme.com/zinedine-zidane/|archive-date=16 April 2015|postscript=. The same interview also appeared in the May 2012 issue of ''Esquire España''.}}</ref> In December 2009 and March 2010 interviews, Zidane had said that he would "rather die than apologise" to Materazzi for the headbutt in the final,<ref>{{cite news|date=1 March 2010|title=Zinedine Zidane says he would 'rather die' than apologise to Marco Materazzi|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/mar/01/zinedine-zidane-marco-materazzi-headbutt|work=The Guardian|access-date=1 July 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/749212/ce/uk/?cc=5739&ver=global|title=Zidane: I'd 'rather die' than say sorry|date=1 March 2010|website=ESPN SoccerNet|access-date=1 March 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100304145802/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/749212/ce/uk/?cc=5739&ver=global|archive-date=4 March 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref> but also admitted that he "could never have lived with himself" had he been allowed to remain on the pitch and help France win the match.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/sports/zidane-glad-head-butt-was-punished-in-world-cup-final-idUSTRE5BL17M/|title=Zidane glad head-butt was punished in World Cup final|agency=Reuters|date=22 December 2009|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/zidane-happy-that-he-was-sent-off-1.794822|title=Zidane happy that he was sent off|work=The Irish Times|date=22 December 2009|access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=22 December 2009|url=http://ww38.momento24.com/en/2009/12/22/zidane-is-glad-he-was-sent-off-in-the-2006-world-cup-final/comment-page-1/|title=Zidane is glad he was sent off in 2006 World Cup final|website=ww38.momento24.com|access-date=19 April 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190803202919/http://ww38.momento24.com/en/2009/12/22/zidane-is-glad-he-was-sent-off-in-the-2006-world-cup-final/comment-page-1/|archive-date=3 August 2019|url-status=dead}}{{dead link|date=June 2026}}</ref> In a January 2010 interview with the Italian newspaper ''[[la Repubblica]]'', Materazzi said that both the headbutt and Thierry Henry's hand ball against [[Republic of Ireland national football team|Ireland]], an episode known as "[[Le Hand of Frog]]" that allowed France to qualify to the 2010 World Cup, showed the "disgusting" side of football.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/01/05/sports/soccer/05sportsbriefs-materazzi.html|title=Head Butt By Zidane Still Angers Materazzi|work=The New York Times|date=4 January 2010|access-date=26 June 2026|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100111105149/http://www.nytimes.com/2010/01/05/sports/soccer/05sportsbriefs-materazzi.html|archive-date=11 January 2010}}</ref> In a December 2009 interview with ''[[France Football]]'' magazine, Zidane thought that there had been an over-reaction to Henry's hand ball episode.<ref name="ABC News 2009"/> In April 2024, Materazzi recalled, "I don't like it, because it doesn't do justice to what my career was. That episode should never have happened. In the tension of that final in Berlin, amidst the bickering and insults, Zidane offered me his shirt, and I said no, that I preferred his sister. Then he turned around and reacted as everyone remembers. I never saw Zinedine again."<ref>{{cite news|date=1 April 2024|title=Materazzi: 'La testata di Zidane? Non doveva succedere. Non l'ho mai più rivisto'|url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Calcio/Nazionale/01-04-2024/materazzi-la-testata-di-zidane-non-doveva-succedere-non-l-ho-mai-piu-rivisto.shtml|work=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

===Criticism of Zidane and philosophical analysis===
On 10 July, despite his violent gesture during the final and his verbal confrontation with referee [[Jorge Larrionda]] during the semi-final against Portugal,<ref>{{cite news|last=Doyle|first=Paul|date=20 July 2006|title=Selfish Zidane has fooled Fifa|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2006/jul/20/newsstory.sport7|work=The Guardian|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=14 July 2006|title=Zidane y Materazzi tendrán otro round|url=https://www.eluniverso.com/2006/07/14/0001/15/A0B3E4FAC947499BB9B80C8776E421A5.html|work=El Universo|language=es|access-date=30 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180509094922/https://www.eluniverso.com/2006/07/14/0001/15/A0B3E4FAC947499BB9B80C8776E421A5.html|archive-date=9 May 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> Zidane was nevertheless elected the best player of the World Cup tournament; this sparked controversy regarding an award that for critics seemed to have been "won from the start" for sponsorship reasons, particularly because Italian midfielder Andrea Pirlo had been named Man of the Match three times (two more than Zidane) during the tournament.<ref>{{cite web|date=10 July 2006|title=Mondiali: Zidane miglior giocatore|url=http://www.tgcom24.mediaset.it/mondiali/articoli/articolo318100.shtml|website=TGcom24|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131019131307/http://www.tgcom24.mediaset.it/mondiali/articoli/articolo318100.shtml|archive-date=19 October 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> Notably, after the final, President Jacques Chirac hailed Zidane as a "man of heart and conviction".<ref>{{cite news|last=Boyle|first=Jon|date=9 July 2006|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/07/09/AR2006070900550.html|title=French fans praise Zidane despite red card|work=The Washington Post|access-date=11 July 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2021|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref>{{cite conference|last=Richard|first=Arnaud|date=July 2007<!-- 15 October 2008 -->|title=Zinedine Zidane: More Than a Mythological Figure, A Prophet|conference=Congrès International de ISHPES et de l'ISSA: Sport in a Global World: Past, Present and Future|location=Copenhague|url=https://hal.science|id=hal-00331225v1|access-date=25 June 2026}}</ref> Chirac defended Zidane against critics by saying that "France loves Zidane", further elaborating: "You are a virtuoso, a genius of world football. You are also a man of heart, commitment, and conviction. That is why France admires and loves you."<ref name="La Gazzetta dello Sport 2006">{{cite news|date=10 July 2006|title=Chirac: 'La Francia ama Zidane'|url=http://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Mondiale_2006/Primo_Piano/2006/07_Luglio/10/chirac.shtml|website=La Gazzetta dello Sport|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> Chirac later added that he found the offence to be unacceptable, but he understood that Zidane had been provoked.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://msnbc.msn.com/id/13857671/|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120712133912/http://msnbc.msn.com/id/13857671/|url-status=dead|archive-date=12 July 2012|title=Chirac calls Zidane head-butt 'unacceptable'|publisher=[[MSNBC]]|date=14 July 2006|access-date=18 March 2008}}</ref>

The French public showed support for Zidane's actions; polls done in the immediate wake of the incident showed 61% of French people said they had already forgiven him for his actions while 52% said they understood them.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/mar/11/world-cup-moments-zinedine-zidane-head-butt|title=World Cup: 25 stunning moments ... No5: Zinedine Zidane's head-butt|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201118143748/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2014/mar/11/world-cup-moments-zinedine-zidane-head-butt|archive-date=18 November 2020|work=The Guardian|location=London|access-date=16 May 2013}}</ref> France manager Raymond Domenech said it was "sad to see him finish like this", saying that Zidane had been "the protagonist of a great career and an excellent World Cup". He added, "When a player has to endure what he endured for so many minutes, without the referee taking action, what happened is understandable. It is not excusable, but it is understandable."<ref>{{cite web|date=11 November 2022|title=Coppa del Mondo 2006: il canto del cigno di Zidane|url=https://www.fifa.com/it/articles/coppa-del-mondo-2006-il-canto-del-cigno-di-zidane|publisher=FIFA|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref> The French press was not as forgiving of its champion; for the French sports newspaper ''[[L'Équipe]]'', Zidane's gesture was far more serious than the defeat of the ''Bleus''. The sports daily wrote, addressing the French captain, "This morning, the hardest part is not trying to understand why the ''Bleus'' lost a World Cup final within their grasp, but explaining to tens of millions of children around the world how you could let yourself strike that headbutt at Materazzi."<ref name="La Gazzetta dello Sport 2006"/> Moreover, the French newspaper ''[[Le Figaro]]'' called the headbutt "odious" and "unacceptable".<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.utvlive.com/newsroom/indepth.asp?id=32550&pt=s|title=French media condemns Zidane|publisher=UTV|date=11 July 2006|access-date=18 March 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080225053701/http://www.utvlive.com/newsroom/indepth.asp?id=32550&pt=s|archive-date=25 February 2008|url-status=dead}}</ref> ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' magazine regarded the incident as a symbol for Europe's "grappling with multi-culturalism".<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Karon|first=Tony|date=13 July 2006|url=http://www.time.com/time/world/article/0,8599,1213502,00.html|title=The Head Butt Furore: A Window on Europe's Identity Crisis|magazine=Time|access-date=18 March 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080603154512/http://www.time.com/time/world/article/0,8599,1213502,00.html|archive-date=3 June 2008|url-status=dead}}</ref> Despite the ongoing furore, Zidane's sponsors announced that they would stick with him.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/money/media/2006-07-11-zidane-ads_x.htm|title=Sponsors stick with Zidane despite head-butt|work=USA Today|date=11 July 2006|access-date=18 March 2008|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080222023117/http://www.usatoday.com/money/media/2006-07-11-zidane-ads_x.htm|archive-date=22 February 2008|url-status=live}}</ref>

Zidane's headbutt was analysed philosophically, leading to a body of mythological literature.<ref name="Baetens 2006"/> The Belgian writer and filmmaker [[Jean-Philippe Toussaint]], who was present at the stadium in Berlin during the final, focused particularly on Zidane's melancholy, writing that "it is my melancholy, I know it, I have nurtured it, I feel it."<ref>{{cite book|last=Toussaint|first=Jean-Philippe|date=2006|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=UhIsAQAAIAAJ|title=La mélancolie de Zidane|location=Paris|publisher=Les Éditions de Minuit|language=fr|page=15|isbn=978-2-7073-1999-9|access-date=2 July 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref> For Toussaint, the headbutt was a creative surge from the artist faced with the looming risk of failure, a "calligraphic gesture", and a stroke of genius that projected the artist Zidane out of and beyond the game of football, turning an imminent defeat into a victory. Toussaint wrote that although he was unable to "score a goal", Zidane "will score minds".<ref>{{cite book|last=Toussaint|first=Jean-Philippe|date=2006|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=UhIsAQAAIAAJ|title=La Mélancolie de Zidane|location=Paris|publisher=Les Éditions de Minuit|language=fr|page=17|isbn=978-2-7073-1999-9}}</ref> For the French sociologist and philosopher [[Jean Baudrillard]], the gesture constituted an act of "[[sabotage]]", a "terrorist" act—meant in quotation marks—against the homogenising dominance of [[globalisation]]. According to Baudrillard, with that gesture, Zidane allegedly refused to seal the [[nothingness]] staged by the "planetary identification ritual" that celebrated the ideology of globalisation as the supreme [[good]] through the media that night.<ref>{{cite book|last=Baudrillard|first=Jean|date=2008|chapter=Le mal ventriloque|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=4PQkAQAAIAAJ|title=Carnaval et cannibale. Suivi de Le Mal ventriloque|location=Paris|publisher=L'Herne|language=fr|page=94|isbn=978-2-85197-862-2|access-date=2 July 2026|via=Google Books}}</ref> Professor Franck Baetens wrote that Zidane's headbutt ultimately did not deny but rather recomposed the symbolic universe in which it takes place, arguing that "the 'footballer's' final gesture ... , far from shattering the myth, actually fulfills it."<ref name="Baetens 2006"/>

In December 2009, major international media outlets ranked the incident at the top of the most iconic sporting moments of the 2000s; [[Reuters]] placed the headbutt at the top of the sports episodes of the decade and included the photograph of the incident in its retrospective of the 100 pictures that defined the decade, labeling it as "the headbutt of the decade".<ref name="Reuters 2009"/> While ''[[The Guardian]]'' labeled it the single most iconic act of sporting villainy of the decade, Reuters highlighted it as a defining event that shaped the sports retrospectives of the 2000s.<ref name="Reuters 2009"/><ref name="Bandini & Stafford 2009"/><ref name="ABC News 2009"/>

===In popular culture===
As it was ultimately watched by billions of people,<ref>{{cite news|last=Brotman|first=Barbara|date=16 July 2006|title=Headbutt is a longtime, if primitive, male tradition|url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/2006/07/16/headbutt-is-a-longtime-if-primitive-male-tradition/|url-status=dead|work=Chicago Tribune|access-date=26 June 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260625230938/https://www.chicagotribune.com/2006/07/16/headbutt-is-a-longtime-if-primitive-male-tradition/|archive-date=25 June 2026}}</ref> with numerous analyses by the media,<ref>{{cite radio|url=https://www.npr.org/2006/07/15/5559572/why-did-zidane-lose-his-cool|title=Why Did Zidane Lose His Cool?|host=Scott Simon|website=NPR|date=15 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.popmatters.com/060718-zinedine-zidane-2496108461.html|title=Z/Z: The Headbutt Buzz|website=PopMatters|date=18 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last1=Schoon|first1=Eric|last2=Oatley|first2=Thomas|title=Headlining the head-butt: Zinedine Zidane/Marco Materazzi portrayals in prominent English, Irish, and Scottish newspapers|url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/240707946_Headlining_the_head-butt_Zinedine_ZidaneMarco_Materazzi_portrayals_in_prominent_English_Irish_and_Scottish_newspapers|journal=Media, Culture & Society|date=1 May 2011|volume=33|issue=4|pages=579–595|doi=10.1177/0163443711398903|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> the incident was extensively lampooned on the [[List of Internet phenomena|Internet]] and in popular culture.<ref>{{cite news|last=Noveck|first=Jocelyn|date=13 July 2006|title=Zidane's headbutt is all the rage in cyberspace|url=https://mg.co.za/news/sport/2006-07-13-zidane-s-headbutt-is-all-the-rage-in-cyberspace/|work=Mail & Guardian|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> ''[[Family Guy]]'' parodied it in the episode "[[Saving Private Brian]]", in which Zidane headbutts an old lady while delivering her a birthday cake.<ref>{{cite news|last=Davies|first=Amanda|date=27 September 2012|title='Ode to defeat': Zidane headbutt immortalized in bronze statue|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2012/09/27/sport/football/zidane-head-butt-statue-football|work=CNN|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="Sunderland 2015">{{cite web|last=Sunderland|first=Tom|date=21 April 2015|title=Ronaldo and 10 Other Footballers Who Have Made It on Screen|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2433855-ronaldo-and-10-other-footballers-who-have-made-it-on-screen|website=Bleacher Report|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> ''[[The Simpsons]]'' parodied it in the episode "[[Marge Gamer]]", in which [[Homer Simpson]] shouts "Zidane!" when headbutting the linesman,<ref>{{cite episode|title=Marge Gamer|series=The Simpsons|credits=Written by J. Stewart Burns; directed by Bob Anderson|network=Fox|location=Los Angeles|season=18|number=17|minutes=01:21|id=JABF10|date=22 April 2007}}</ref> an episode that also saw a cameo from Brazil's [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]].<ref name="Sunderland 2015"/> As of December 2009, "Zinedine Zidane headbutt" was a common [[Google Search]] inquiry when typing his name.<ref name="Bandini & Stafford 2009"/> In addition to becoming a staple of parody via numerous online videos and [[GIF]]s,<ref>{{cite news|title=The new (head-)butt of Internet jokes|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna13834112|work=NBC News|date=12 July 2006|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref> as well as T-shirts, video games, and books,<ref name="ABC News 2009"/> a [[novelty song]] titled "[[Coup de Boule]]" (French for "headbutt") reached the top of the French charts.<ref>{{cite news|last=Arendt|first=Paul|date=1 August 2006|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2006/aug/01/1|title=Zidane headbutt single storms the French charts|work=The Guardian|access-date=26 June 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Zidane's head-butt song tops French charts|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/newsitems/200608/s1703710.htm|publisher=ABC News Australia|date=2 August 2006|access-date=6 September 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090306114810/http://www.abc.net.au/news/newsitems/200608/s1703710.htm|archive-date=6 March 2009|url-status=live}}</ref> ''[[Headbutt (sculpture)|Headbutt]]'' ({{langx|fr|Coup de tête|link=no}}), a sculpture of the incident, was unveiled in 2012.<ref name=f24>{{cite news|title=Zinedine Zidane headbutt statue unveiled in Paris|url=http://www.france24.com/en/20120926-france-art-football-world-cup-2006-zidane-headbutt-statue-paris-pompidou-centre/|work=France 24|date=26 September 2012|access-date=5 January 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140105223252/http://www.france24.com/en/20120926-france-art-football-world-cup-2006-zidane-headbutt-statue-paris-pompidou-centre/|archive-date=5 January 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> Besides the headbutt, books about the final or to celebrate Italy's triumph continue to be written 20 years after the events, such as ''Gli ultimi campioni. Il romanzo dei mondiali 2006'', written by Luigi Garlando and published by Turin's UTET.<ref>{{cite web|date=4 November 2022|title=I migliori libri sul calcio e sui Mondiali (da leggere e regalare)|url=https://www.illibraio.it/liste-di-libri/libri-calcio-mondiali/|website=IlLibraio.it|language=it|access-date=30 June 2026}}</ref>

